# Metal–Organic Frameworks and Their Derivatives as Cathodes for Lithium-Ion Battery Applications: A Review

R. Chenna Krishna Reddy1 $\cdot$ Xiaoming Lin1 $\textcircled{1}$ · Akif Zeb1 $\cdot$ Cheng‑Yong Su2
Abstract
The development of energy storage technology is important for resolving the issues and challenges of utilizing sustainable green energy in modern-day society. As an emerging technology, lithium-ion batteries (LIBs) are a common source of power for a wide variety of electronic devices, and major advances require the development and exploitation of new electrode materials; thus, fundamental knowledge of their atomic and nanoscale properties is necessary. By moving beyond conventional cathode candidates, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) chemistry provides an excellent direction for designing and developing promising high-performance cathode materials for use in LIBs. Here, we carry out an overarching discussion on the development and application of MOFs and their derivatives as cathodes for lithium-ion battery applications. A timely overview of the exciting progress of MOFs as well as MOF-derived metallic components is highlighted. The unique characteristics of MOFs, such as their large surface area, high tunable porosity with uniform pore size, unique structural and morphological features, controllable framework composition and low densities, combine together to provide good interfacial charge transport properties and short diffusion lengths for electrons and/or ions that adequately support electrochemical redox reactions. The progress of MOFs and their derived composites as cathode candidates for LIBs is emphasized based on their electrochemical results, while also discussing the remaining issues and potential upcoming research directions.
Keywords  MOFs and their derived derivatives $\cdot\cdot$ Li-ion batteries $\cdot\cdot$ Cathodes $\cdot\cdot$ Lithium storage
## 1  Introduction
After being commercialized, lithium-ion batteries (LIBs) have become more popular as a power source for various devices in the portable electronic market and occupy an integral part of life in modern society. Currently, the application of LIBs has moved beyond portable electronics, transitioning into the transportation sector to power vehicles; the aim is to reduce the current dependency on fossil fuel-based energy sources to alleviate global climate issues and to encourage the development and use of sustainable green energy sources. Significant improvements have been achieved in the development of LIB systems, including addressing safety concerns and increasing power density; thus, these LIB systems have been extensively applied in modern electric automobiles. To satisfy increasing market demands, many countries are carrying out research for reaching preset energy density targets. In China, the driving range capacity target of electric vehicles (EVs) per charge is $400\mathrm{km}$ in 2020, and the energy density (amount of energy stored per unit volume or mass) target is $350\mathrm{Wh}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ and $250\mathrm{Wh}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ for cell-level and pack-level LIB systems, respectively [1].
The Nobel Prize awarded to John Goodenough, Stanley Whittingham and Akira Yoshino in 2019 in chemistry strengthened the resolution of researchers to establish efficient and sustainable renewable energy technology in daily life, thus bringing about a sustainable energy revolution. The present status of this young technology has encouraged concerted efforts in solid-state chemistry during the last 50 years, resulting in many new materials being developed as battery components, which has enhanced technology [2]. Owing to the easy availability, renewability, cost effectiveness and environmental friendliness of LIBs, there has been an emphasis on creating numerous innovative electrode materials for battery applications. The main focus of electrode development has mainly been concentrated on simple, cost-effective synthesis protocols, enhanced electrochemical performance and increased safety in practice. Moreover, efforts have been made to provide high energy densities and large current outputs in LIBs, which in turn reduce the price of ecofriendly electric vehicles (EVs) and many smart electronic gadgets [3, 4]. The main components of a battery are the anode, cathode and electrolyte, and the battery is operated individually or with a combination of insertion, conversion and alloying mechanisms [5]. In particular, cathodes with layered structures are crucial among all battery components due to their lithiation/delithiation ability; thus, they have become an active research hotspot for advancing energy storage devices to satisfy future energy demands [6, 7].
The use of oxide-based layered cathode active materials, such as $\mathbf{LiCoO}_{2}$ [8], $\mathrm{Li}(\mathrm{Ni}_{x}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{y}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{o}_{z})\mathbf{O}_{2}$ [9, 10], $\mathrm{Li}(\mathrm{Ni}_{1-y-z}\mathrm{Co}_{y}\mathrm{Al}_{z})\mathrm{O}_{2}$ [11, 12], spinel oxide cathodes, such as $\mathrm{LiNi}_{y}\mathrm{Mn}_{2\ensuremath{\mathbf{\cdot}}y}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ [13, 14], or polyanion oxide cathodes, such as ${\mathrm{LiFePO}}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{FeP}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}$ [15], in advanced lithiumion batteries has been demonstrated as a potential solution for sustainable energy storage systems that can power EVs and compete with gasoline-based vehicular transportation. Although metal oxides perform well owing to their abundance of redox-active sites and sturdy structure, they have a few drawbacks, which hinder their large-scale commercial utilization for future energy demands. Some of the drawbacks of the above materials are (1) their structural flaws, which allow inferior lithium-ion accommodation and lower capacity than their theoretical values due to the formation of interfacial insulating metal-oxide layers [16]; (2) the severe degradation of cathode material/large volumetric changes during long-term cycling; (3) $\mathbf{LiCoO}_{2}$ facing safety-related issues due to the release of oxygen; (4) mediocre electronic and ionic diffusion and their relatively low operating voltages; and (5) harsh reaction conditions, which make LIBs expensive because of their processing steps (an initial hightemperature reaction and postsynthetic modifications) and make scaling-up difficult [17]. To address these issues and to strengthen the performance of cathode materials, several synthesis modifications (nanoparticle synthesis [18], conductive carbon coating [19], elemental doping [20] and structural reconstruction [21]) have been adapted, and the use of inexpensive, abundant materials (for example, Fe and Mn instead of Co) has been explored to reduce the battery cost with the aim of reaching a high specific energy density of $500\ \mathrm{Wh\kg^{-1}}$ in battery packs by 2030. This value is higher than twice the specific energy density of current EV batteries $(170{-}200\ \mathrm{Wh}\ \mathrm{kg}^{-1})$ [22]. In addition, organicbased cathode materials with reversible redox-active functional groups and flexible structures, which permit an ultrafast charging speed, have also been investigated. However, their dissolution in electrolytes and large molecular weight per electron uptake make them less considerable [16, 23].
Over the past 20  years, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) have been explicitly explored as a new kind of porous material and have gained extensive significance owing to their stable and controllable framework structure, controllable topological diversity, high tunable porosity, large specific surface area and low framework density, thus unlocking many prospects in solving problems related to sustainable energy storage systems [24]. The two major components of a typical hybrid inorganic–organic framework are metal ions/clusters of lanthanides, alkaline earth metals or transition metals combined with organic ligands/ linkers with O- or N-donor atoms to form an open framework structure. The commonly used organic ligands are bi-, tri-, tetra- or multidentate ligands, such as carboxylates (which became more famous through the research works of O. M. Yaghi, the pioneer of MOF chemistry), pyridyl and polyamines have become increasingly studied [25]. Although the development and research activities/interests related to MOF chemistry started 20 years back, they are still in the discovery phase because there is considerable space for the design and development of new MOFs by regulating organic ligand struts and metal-ion nodes. As per the Cambridge Structural Database (CSD), the number of MOFs was 20000 in 2013 and increased to 88000 in 2018, disclosing the extensive investigation of these materials for use in various fields over traditional porous materials, such as zeolites, microporous phosphates and ordered mesoporous silica. The variety of possibilities for the self-assembled coordination of metal nodes with organic linkers has provided: (1) a facile route to gain knowledge regarding rational designs; (2) the control of synthesis conditions toward achieving desirable structural properties, thereby attaining structure–property–performance relationships; (3) the thermodynamics and kinetics of MOF crystal growth; (4) and finally leveraging the specific application of MOFs in science and engineering fields [26, 27].
Moreover, the utilization of MOFs as sacrificial templates/precursors through deliberately controlled postsynthesis methods (ion swapping, surface coating, thermal treatments, removing organic linker/metal ions, etc.) enables the design and development of a vast number of MOF-derived materials with desirable composition and morphology [28]. Therefore, in addition to the alteration of the structural properties and/or chemical functionalities of MOFs for targeted applications, the fabrication of MOF-derived carbons and MOF-derived transition metal derivatives via the modulation of parent MOFs may certainly expand new opportunities. The temperature-controlled postsynthesis method is famous for the synthesis of MOF-derived carbons with or without heteroatom doping (N, S and P) in which the carbon content is high in addition to exhibiting an inherent open-framework structure, high surface area and uniformly distributed pores. Again, highly conductive porous carbon-based materials with defects possess distinct electronic properties that are capable of electrochemical reactions; these reactions are facilitated by an abundance of active sites and good surface charge redistribution. Similarly, MOF-derived transition metal derivatives obtained through thermal treatments inherit the morphology, high surface area and adjustable porosity features of pristine MOFs, which are highly desirable for electrochemical energy storage and conversion applications. A predesigned strategy with good yields using inexpensive precursors reduces the cost of targeted energyrelated applications on a large scale [26, 29].
Among excellent cathode candidates, MOFs and their derived active materials have shown promising electrochemical behavior and electron/ion storage properties in rechargeable lithium-ion batteries via reversible lithium-ion intercalation/deintercalation, conversion and alloying mechanisms. In 2003, Cheng et al. reported a novel inorganic–organic hybrid architecture that contained vanadium and gallium centers, i.e., $[\mathrm{Ga}_{2}(\mathrm{VO})_{3}\mathrm{K}_{2}(\mathrm{OH}_{2})_{3}(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{6})_{4}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{13}]$ , termed as NCHU-3 (National Chung-Hsing University-3). The redox oxovanadyl center of the new cathode material provided an intercalation host for lithium ions in Li-ion batteries [30]. In 2006, Li et al. reported a $Z\mathrm{n}$ -based MOF (MOF-177) for reversible lithium storage; however, the results were not quite impressive, and the authors claimed that “MOF-177 is not suitable for application in reversible lithium storage” [31]. However, afterward, the poor reversible lithium storage reactions were manipulated and improved by altering the valence of the metal ions. An iron compound with the composition $\mathrm{Fe^{HI}(O H)}_{0.8}\mathrm{F}_{0.2}(\mathsf{b d c})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ , (bdc stands for 1,4-benzenedicarboxylate) known as MIL53 (MIL stands for Materials of Institute Lavoisier) with classes II and III mixed-valence states is the first example of an MOF employed as a promising cathode for lithiumbased batteries [32]. Since then, a myriad number of novel MOFs and their derived materials have been investigated and explicitly explored as positive electrode materials for rechargeable Li-ion batteries. More recently, several reviews have investigated the active role of MOFs and their derived materials in various rechargeable batteries [33, 34]. However, herein, we particularly focus on their application in Liion batteries, which may provide an instructive and knowledgeable resource for MOF redox chemistry by providing insights into advancing research on next-generation lithiumion batteries. Various significant efforts have been made to apply MOFs and MOF-derived candidates as cathodes in lithium-ion batteries to provide high energy densities and large current outputs that can power ecofriendly electric vehicles (EVs) and smart electronic gadgets (Fig. 1). By discussing the design principles and synthesis strategies of MOFs and their derived composites as cathode candidates in LIBs, we also emphasize the timely overview of the exciting progress of their electrochemical performances in relation to their structure–composition–electrochemical performance relationships. By the end of the review, a perspective on the remaining issues and possible upcoming research directions will be discussed.
## 2  Synthesis Strategies for the Preparation of LIB Cathode Materials
Metal–organic frameworks/porous coordination polymers (MOFs/PCPs) are synthetic solid materials possessing porous framework structures that have furthered great advances in heterogeneous catalysis, gas storage, membrane separation, molecular sensing, medical, and energy storage and conversion applications over the last 50 years. To reach targeted applications, these MOFs respond well to structural and crystal engineering to obtain desirable physical and chemical properties. There have been many reviews covering existing knowledge for the possible synthesis of desirable MOF materials in regard to several synthesis parameters, such as temperature, pressure, solvent, $\mathrm{\pH}$ and reaction time. Silva et al. documented various synthesis methods used to prepare MOFs (for example, slow diffusion, hydrothermal, solvothermal, electrochemical, mechanochemical, microwave-assisted heating, ultrasonic, one-pot) by mentioning their advantages and disadvantages [41]. The above review also summarized the basic concepts of the engineering, industrial production and direct application of MOFs in society. Seoane et al. highlighted the latest advances in MOF crystal engineering by discussing the fundamental aspects of solution-based nucleation and crystal growth that occurs during MOF crystallization [42]. Therefore, herein, we briefly discuss the synthesis methods that have been adapted by researchers to synthesize MOFs and their derived nanostructures for application as cathode materials in lithium-ion batteries. For a better understanding, the synthesis methods, composition, morphology and sizes are presented in Table 1. Based on our knowledge, we have discussed all the synthesis methods used so far for the production of pristine MOF cathodes, MOF-mediated surface-modified cathodes and MOF-derived nanostructure cathodes and believe that this review will be beneficial as a reference to researchers who are working on MOFs and their derivatives in electrochemistry to design and construct new types of electrode materials for better electrochemical properties.
Initially, Ferey et al. used a solvothermal process to synthesize the MIL-53(Fe) cathode material [32]. Their typical procedure involved equimolar amounts of ${\mathrm{Fe}}(\mathrm{ClO}_{4})_{3}{\cdot}x{\mathrm{H}}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , 1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid and hydrofluoric acid in DMF $(\mathrm{N},\mathrm{N^{\prime}}$ -dimethylformamide) that were added together and stirred for a few minutes before being transferred to a Teflon-lined steel autoclave. The sample was kept at $423\mathrm{~K~}$ for 3 days to obtain MIL-53(Fe)_ $_{\mathrm{{H}}_{2}\mathrm{{O}}}$ . Similarly, a hydrothermal reaction method was used to prepare MIL-116(Fe), in which $\mathrm{FeCl}_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , mellitic acid and LiCl were added to $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of water and kept for $20\mathrm{h}$ at $180^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in a Teflon-lined autoclave [43]. It was observed that most of the Prussian blue analogs (PBAs) were synthesized either by precipitation, coprecipitation or heterogeneous precipitation methods, which involved the dropwise addition of aqueous reactants to one another with constant stirring at room temperature [44–46].
Jacobson’s group reported a reflux method to obtain the yellow brown precipitate of ${\mathrm{V}}^{\mathrm{IV}}({\mathrm{O}})$ (bdc) [47]. Hydrated vanadyl sulfate and diammonium benzenedicarboxylate were dissolved in $20~\mathrm{mL}$ of dimethylformamide and refluxed at $160^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 3 days using an oil bath with a round-bottomed flask that was fitted with a condenser. The product was filtered and washed with methanol before being dried under vacuum at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{h}$ . A simple room-temperature magnetically stirred reaction was adapted to synthesize hydrated reduced graphene oxide/metal–organic phosphate open framework (rGO/MOPOF), i.e., $\mathrm{rGO/K}_{2}[(\mathrm{VO})_{2}(\mathrm{HPO}_{4})_{2}(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4})]$ , which was followed by drying at $90~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ [48]. Additionally, the authors reported the synthesis of $\mathrm{K}_{2}[(\mathrm{VO})_{2}(\mathrm{HPO}_{4})_{2}(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4})]$ using mechanical grinding and sonochemical reactions. Liquid-phase step-by-step growth was developed by Makiura et al. to synthesize Prussian blue nanolayers (PBNLs) grown on the surface of $\mathbf{LiCoO}_{2}$ (LCO) particles $(\mathrm{PBNL}@\mathrm{LCO})$ for use as a cathode material [49]. This sequential growth involved the initial dispersion of $\mathbf{LiCoO}_{2}$ to isonicotinic acid in ethanol and was stirred for $^{\textrm{1h}}$ . Then, the isonicotinic acidattached LCO particles were added to $\mathrm{FeCl}_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ aqueous solution and stirred for $10\mathrm{min}$ and filtered. Again, these LCO particles were added to a ${\bf K}_{3}[\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]$ aqueous solution and stirred for $10~\mathrm{{min}}$ to obtain PBNL $@\mathrm{LCO}$ after being dried under vacuum for one night (Fig. 2).
Li et al. reported an effective surface modification for $\mathrm{LiNi_{0.6}C o_{0.2}M n_{0.2}O_{2}}$ (NCM-622) by using an MOF precursor $(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{MIL}-53)$ to produce MOF-derived alumina (MDA) coatings [37]. First, NCM-622 was prepared using the coprecipitation method, and then a solvothermal approach was used to prepare the ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}{\mathrm{-MIL}}-53$ precursor. Next, amorphous alumina was well dispersed on the surface of NCM-622 through a facile mechanochemical and thermal treatment to obtain $\mathbf{MDA@NCM}$ . This surface modification helped to protect the active electrode material from direct contact with the electrolyte, which facilitated the easy transport of ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ . In another surface modification, the sol–gel method was used to synthesize Li-rich layered $\mathrm{Li_{1.2}M n_{0.54}N i_{0.13}C o_{0.13}O_{2}}$ oxide (LLO) nanoparticles that were uniformly mixed with 2-methylimidazole [38]. This mixture was subjected to a low-pressure vapor superassembly process and finally calcined to obtain the final product, NiCo nanodot-decorated carbon-confined $\mathrm{Li}_{1.2}\mathrm{Mn}_{0.54}\mathrm{Ni}_{0.13}\mathrm{Co}_{0.13}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ nanoparticles (LLO $@$ C&NiCo) (Fig.  3a). A microwave-assisted hydrothermal synthesis method was designed and developed by Cheng et al. for the synthesis of Fe-bdc MOFs, and based on the microwave irradiation time, two types of Fe-bdc MOFs were formed, as shown in Fig. 3b [50]. These octahedral and spindle-like Fe-bdc MOFs were then subjected to low-temperature fluorination in a tube furnace at $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{3\mathrm{h}}$ in a $\Nu_{2}$ atmosphere to obtain their respective $\mathrm{FeF}_{3}{\cdot}0.33\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ nanostructures that retained the original morphologies of the precursors.
It is well known that the preparation of electrode materials with MOFs as precursors is a well-studied technique and has been increasingly investigated because of the inheritance of the framework properties, superior structural diversity, wellretained porosities and high specific surface areas of the precursor MOFs. This solid-state approach, which uses controlled thermal treatments of pristine MOFs, has led to the formation of not only metal fluorides [50] but also metal oxides, metal sulfides, metal phosphides and metal selenides when treated with the corresponding gaseous components. In addition, the MOF-templated synthesis of porous carbonaceous materials is also worth pursuing. Foley et al. reported the initial synthesis of HKUST-1 (HKUST represents Hong Kong University of Science and Technology) using a solvothermal approach, and upon a subsequent heat treatment in the presence of S at $500^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ , HKUST-1 was then converted to $\mathrm{Cu}_{1.8}\mathrm{S}/\mathrm{C}$ [51]. Hybrid vanadium metal–organic frameworks have been synthesized by using wastewater as organic ligands (because wastewater contains various kinds of solvents, emulsifiers, lipids, dissolved organic matter, etc.) through a hydrothermal assisted sol–gel method [39]. After drying the precursor in air at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , calcination is performed to obtain the final cathode material, $\mathrm{Li}_{3}\mathrm{V}_{2}(\mathrm{PO}_{4})_{3}/$ phosphorus-doped carbon (LVP/P–C) nanocomposites (Fig. 4). Bimetallic Fe–Co-ZIF (ZIF stands for zeolitic imidazolate frameworks) templates have been synthesized via the room-temperature stirring method and calcining at $700^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ; in this case, the organic ligand generates conductive three-dimensional (3D) porous graphitized carbon frameworks [52]. Later, the mixture of annealed product and $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ is again copyrolyzed at $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to obtain the final hybrid $\mathrm{CoF}_{2}/\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ cathode material.
Among various synthesis strategies, the surface modification of commercial cathodes with MOFs through mechanochemical methods and a subsequent annealing step has demonstrated fruitful results toward improved cathodic performance in LIBs. In addition, the MOF template synthesis method is also a widely used technique for fabricating various nanostructures with metal components; however, the release of volatile and/or toxic gases with this method needs to be properly handled. Some research groups have used a combination of different synthesis methods to achieve highefficiency cathode materials (Table 1). Thus, it is envisaged that the implementation and promotion of green synthesis protocols for MOFs through the use of less toxic transition metal ions (for example, zinc and iron), green organic ligands (for example, tartaric acid, citric acid, fumaric acid, etc.) and biomass-derived solvents are worthwhile and have current applicability in science and technology.
## 3  Pristine MOFs as Cathode Materials for LIBs
MOFs are classic micro- and/or mesoporous solid materials that provide overwhelming support as battery components in the area of energy storage and conversion through their adjustable structure-related properties. Fortunately, due to the existence of central metal ions with redox behavior and organic ligands with charge storage and transfer ability, MOFs can be used as redox-active materials in electrochemical processes. Furthermore, their porosity is also conducive to the migration of $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ . Additionally, the structural stability of MOFs can also ensure the stable performance of lithiumion batteries during cycling. Due to their large number of unique structures, highly ordered micro- and/or mesopores and selection of metal ions that can coordinate with an abundance of organic ligands, MOFs have a variety of composition, which in turn makes them more useful than other porous materials. MOFs can be directly used as positive electrode materials for electrochemical energy storage and conversion in Li-ion batteries. In this case, considerable progress has been achieved, and a good number of MOFs have been studied as LIB cathodes. These MOFs contain an even distribution of abundant pores as well as redox-active sites on both the metallic species and organic functional moieties that can provide sufficient lithium storage sites, subsequently relieving the irreversible volume changes during charge/discharge processes. Thus, we discuss these MOFs in detail and categorize this section into iron-based MOFs as cathode materials and other transition metal-based MOFs as cathode materials for lithium-ion battery applications.
## 3.1  Iron‑Based MOFs as Cathode Materials
Generally, the low electropositive nature and partial occupation of the d orbitals of transition metal elements are favorable for directional covalent bonds. Iron, as a benign transition element with a valence electron configuration of $3\mathrm{d}^{6}4\mathrm{s}^{2}$ , exhibits compounds that act as an attracting semiconductor with electrical and magnetic properties [59]. The modulation of material properties associated with adjustments in chemical composition and porosity is highly recommended to attain better applicability [60].
Of note, a substantial number of iron-containing metal–organic frameworks (Fe-based MOFs) have been developed and studied as cathode materials for rechargeable batteries owing to the plentiful resources of iron $(5.1\%$ crust quality), along with its low toxicity, redox activity and biodegradability. The feasibility of combining unsaturated Fe(III) centers with organic ligand oxygen atoms generates a strong Fe–O coordination bond, which in turn stabilizes the porous framework structure and improves their potential applicability in LIBs. In addition, the majority of Fe-based MOFs comprise the iron series in the following categories: MILs (Materials of Institute Lavoisier) and PB and PBAs (Prussian blue and Prussian blue analogs). However, in PB and PBAs, a coordination bond is formed between unsaturated, multivalent metal ions and cyanide ligands [61].
An early successful report that demonstrated a new kind of MOF cathode material in lithium-ion batteries was on MIL-53 (Fe), which was developed by Tarascon et al. in 2007. The composition of this intercalation cathode, as per the elemental and thermogravimetric analysis, is $\mathrm{Fe^{HI}(O H)}_{0.8}\mathrm{F}_{0.2}(\mathrm{bdc)}](\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ with iron in the higher oxidation state of $\mathrm{Fe(III)}$ [32]. The idea of design comes from the possibility of inducing mixed-valence states within the MOF moiety, thus affecting their electrochemical reactivity toward Li. The poor reversible reactions of MOFs, i.e., NCHU-3 and $Z\mathrm{n}_{4}\mathrm{O}(1,3,5\$ -benzenetribenzoate) $\vert{_2}$ [30, 31], become better due to 3d transition metals when they take advantage of the low occupation of the 3d-electron orbital, which increases the $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{O}$ bond stability with respect to charge variations and triggers long-range electron delocalization through the stabilization of class II or class III mixed-valence states that are coupled with a double-exchange mechanism. Electrochemical performance investigations revealed that lithium ions, at an amount of approximately $0.6\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ per Fe, were embedded in the structure during reversible lithium insertion/extraction. The reversible reduction/oxidation of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ was accompanied by a first cycle reversible capacity of nearly $75\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $\mathrm{C}/40$ with steady capacity retention of $70\mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ ( $80\%$ of the initial capacity) after 50 cycles without any framework alteration. The ex situ results from Mossbauer spectroscopy and the in situ studies from X-ray absorption fine structure (XAFS) spectroscopy at the Fe K-edge confirmed the reversible intercalation of ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ in MIL-53 accompanied by the reduction of $\mathrm{Fe(III)}$ to $\mathrm{Fe(II)}$ [62].
It has also been observed from further attempts that the irreversible decomposition of MIL-53 (Fe) took place by embedding more than 0.6 Li atoms per unit formula by decreasing the discharge voltage to $0.5\mathrm{V}$ . Evidently, Combelles et al. investigated the redox mechanism of lithium insertion/extraction to explain the decomposition of MIL-53 (Fe) by first-principles density functional theory $(\mathrm{DFT})+\mathrm{U}$ and local chemical bond analysis. To further discuss the electrochemical properties, $\mathrm{Fe(OH)}_{1-y}\mathrm{F}_{y}(\mathrm{bdc)}$ , which contained hydroxyl bridging ligands replaced by a fluorine atom, showed improved capacities when compared to the fluorine-free electrode, and it was stated that the total reversible capacity increased as a function of the fluorine content [63]. However, the best electrochemical performance was achieved for $y=0.2$ , and up to ${\sim}0.6\ \mathrm{Li/Fe}$ was successfully and reversibly exchanged between electrodes during the charge and discharge cycles. Qualitatively, the influence of OH/F substitution on the redox properties was discussed by using a simple orbital approach, in which the strong electronegative character of fluorine made the organic ligand less of a donor (more negatively charged) than the oxygen of the $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ group with respect to the transition metal. Thus, the redox properties should be switched on by OH/F replacement due to the stabilization of the metallic 3d orbitals. The fluorine present in the electrode material should stabilize the delocalized mixed-valence state in a two-phase reaction, thus increasing the solid solution domain of the insertion process. Computational results suggested that irreversible decomposition should take place when reaching the critical composition of $x\mathrm{Li}/\mathrm{Fe}>0.5$ , whose driving force is a complete drop in the cohesive interactions between the metal and organic networks of the MOF architecture [62–65].
In continuation of their research for enhancing the electrochemical performance of MOF cathode materials, Tarascon et  al. again reported a new iron-based hybrid MOF material with a 1:1 molar ratio of MIL-53(Fe) as the host and 1,4-benzoquinone as the electroactive guest molecule, and the final composition was named MIL53(Fe)_quinone $_1$ [66]. While examining the ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ insertion capability, the overall amount of lithium that could be embedded into MIL-53(Fe)_quinone $_1$ reached 1.2, which presented a high gravimetric capacity of $93\mathrm{~mAh~g^{-1}}$ ; however, this value rapidly decayed after only five cycles and reached a capacity that equaled that of quinone-free MIL-53(Fe). This poor capacity retention was attributed to the continuous swapping between quinone and dimethyl carbonate (DMC) electrolyte molecules leading to the electrolyte-driven leaching of quinone molecules, which did not allow the capacity value to be maintained during long-term cycling. Notably, the authors stated that ‘the electrochemical performance of quinone-loaded materials turns out to be somewhat disappointing.’ Additionally, the authors reported MIL-68(Fe) as a new LIB MOF cathode material having the formula Fe(OH)(bdc)· $(\mathrm{d}\mathrm{mf})_{x}$ $(x\approx1.1)$ possessed the same inorganic secondary building unit as MIL-53(Fe) but exhibited larger and more rigid pores that appeared to be a capable for additional lithium insertion [67]. However, the amount of lithium inserted per Fe atom in MIL-68(Fe) was approximately 0.35 into the structure at C/10 in the galvanostatic mode, which was less than the value reported for MIL-53(Fe).
Inspired by the fruitful results of the above new research direction, Meng’s group studied and reported the electrochemically active MOF material MIL-101(Fe) as a cathode electrode in Li-ion batteries [35]. The secondary building unit (SBU) of MIL-101(Fe) was composed of a carboxylatebridged, oxo-centered, trinuclear $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ complex, as shown in Fig. 5a. The metal center redox couple $(\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}/\mathrm{Fe}^{2+})$ was electrochemically active and accommodated $0.62\mathrm{Li}/\mathrm{Fe}$ upon initial lithiation when discharged down to $2.0\mathrm{V}.$ This value is close to the previously reported value of MIL-53(Fe) $(\sim0.6$ Li/Fe) and more than the value of MIL-68(Fe) $(\mathrm{\sim}0.35~\mathrm{Li}/\$ Fe). However, MIL-53(Fe) achieved this performance at a lower voltage cutoff of $1.5\mathrm{V}$ , thereby concluding that MIL68(Fe) demonstrated this performance at a higher voltage; this advancement may be due to MIL-68(Fe) having larger pores than MIL-53(Fe). Conversely, the first Coulombic efficiency (CE) of MIL-101(Fe) was $79\%$ , which was less than that of MIL-53(Fe) $(\sim97\%)$ but nearly equal to that of MIL68(Fe) $(\sim79\%)$ . In addition, the results of ex situ and in operando X-ray absorption spectroscopy (XAS) on the Fe K-edge suggested that the redox mechanism was not reversible over many cycles and exhibited diminished capacity retention that hosted only $0.37\mathrm{Li/Fe}$ by the 5th cycle. The authors proposed that this irreversible accumulation of lithium was due to the relaxation of $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ to $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ in MIL-101(Fe), which led to diminished capacity retention. One can also observe from Fig. 5 that the rate of lithiation/delithiation affects reversibility, as shown by the results of 30 cycles at C/60, C/40, C/20 and $\mathbf{C}/10$ rates.
To reinvestigate the application of MIL-101(Fe) as a positive electrode material in Li-ion batteries, Yamada et al. reported the synthesis of MIL-101(Fe) using inexpensive components such as iron, terephthalic acid and hydroxyl or chloride anions. It is pertinent to mention here that the capacitance and cycling performance of electrodes depend upon proper preparation conditions and mixing conditions with carbon or adhesive materials [68]. Hence, the authors mixed the as-synthesized MOF with Ketjen black and polytetrafluoroethylene (PTFE) adhesive and made a coin-shaped pellet $10\mathrm{mm}$ in diameter. Finally, a cell with the MIL-101(Fe) cathode, a separator holding the electrolyte and lithium metal was fabricated and studied for its electrochemical performance. As expected, the as-prepared cathode material incorporated a good quantity of lithium ions per iron atom $(0.62\ \mathrm{Li/Fe})$ and showed good cycling ability for more than 100 cycles due to the clear assistance of the conductive carbon, which maintained a prominent connection with the active material and led to high electrical conductivity. Furthermore, the secondary building unit of $[\mathrm{Fe}_{3}(\mathrm{O}^{2-})(\mathrm{Cl}^{-})(\mathrm{RCOO}^{-})_{6}(\mathrm{L})_{3}]$ with cubic and 3D microporous structures provided the flexibility and reversibility of iron sites toward the insertion of equimolar lithium ions. The authors also pointed out that the poor performance of MIL-53(Fe) and MIL-68(Fe) might be due to the one-dimensional (1D) porous structure. The cycling ability of MIL-101(Fe) was evaluated at a rate of $0.2\mathrm{C}$ , in which the first cycle discharge capacity $(60\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}},$ ) was found to be less than that of the discharge capacity at the 100th cycle $(72\mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}},$ because of the immersion of the whole cathode material in the electrolyte. In addition, the high and stable Coulombic efficiency $(98\%)$ indicated the absence of the decomposition of electrolytes or active electrode material. Afterward, the authors reported a new iron MOF candidate, MIL-116(Fe), synthesized via hydrothermal treatment and tested as a LIB cathode [43]. This MOF synthesis added to the rational design because it was isostructural to MIL116(Al) with trivalent aluminum cation, hydroxo and mellitate ligands. The single-crystal structure of the iron derivative, i.e., MIL-116(Fe), showed high redox activity. However, such redox activity was not observed for the other derivatives with gallium and indium cations. Upon investigating its electrochemical performance, it was found that there was a large capacitance difference between the charge and discharge capacity of the initial 30 cycles due to the irreversible decarboxylation reaction, which led to the oxidative elimination of carboxylate that was associated with the loss in Coulombic efficiency. Subsequent cycles were performed at approximately $100\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ after 60 cycles. At high discharge rates, for example, $10\mathrm{C}$ and $20\mathrm{C}$ , MIL-116(Fe) exhibited redox capacities of $42\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $30\mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively.
Prussian blue (PB) is prepared by a coprecipitation reaction of an iron(III) salt and $\mathrm{[Fe^{II}(C N)_{6}]^{4-}}$ in the presence of water. It is an artificial coordination polymer with a face-centered cubic (fcc) crystal structure that contains alternating iron(III) and iron(II) cations bridged by cyanoligands. The substitution of iron cations is easily possible with other transition metal cations, and the as-generated candidates are termed as Prussian blue analogs (PBAs). PBAs or hexacyanometalates are the classical class of metal–organic framework families with the basic formula $\mathrm{A}_{x}\mathrm{M}_{1}[\mathrm{M}_{2}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]_{y}{\cdot}n\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (A represents alkali metal cations implanted at interstitial spaces of the framework, while $\mathbf{M}_{1}$ and $\mathbf{M}_{2}$ represent transition metals that are the same or different), which have been studied exhaustively for electrochemical energy storage and conversion owing to their advantageous structural, magnetic and electrochemical properties [69]. Moreover, (1) PBA compounds are well known for their structural stability and redox-active potential and (2) their elemental variability with different combinations in a single template moiety. Additionally, (iii) a templatemediated synthesis of PB produces highly crystalline PB and PBA-derived materials with hollow structures, which are crucial for many energy-related applications. As a new cathode material, Okubo et al. reported a Prussian blue analog with the formula $\mathrm{A}_{x}\mathrm{Mn}_{\ y}^{\mathrm{II}}[\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]$ · $n\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ ${(\bf{A};\bf{K}}$ , Rb) for reversible lithium-ion insertion/extraction reactions in LIBs [70]. Upon battery testing, the authors observed that nonaqueous ethylene carbonate and diethyl carbonate electrolytes showed good desolvation during Li-ion insertion/ extraction at the electrode–electrolyte interface, which was not possible for aqueous electrolytes due to the radius of hydrated Li ions being larger than the size of the PBA lattice channel. The charge/discharge capacity for both compounds was almost comparable at approximately $60\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}$ at a current density of $50\mathrm{mAg^{-1}}$ and did not fade even after 100 cycles. The good cathodic performance of the above material might be attributed to the robustness of the Fe–CN–Mn frameworks and strong electron delocalization, including the valence tautomerism. The authors also stated that the redox-active site could be switched by valence tautomerism based on the ex situ X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) results. In addition, the energy density $(\sim200~\mathrm{Wh~kg^{-1})}$ was comparable to that of MIL-53, despite the capacity of MIL-53 being high $(75\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}},$ ) because the reversible Li-ion insertion/ extraction voltage of the above PBA is higher $(3.3~\mathrm{V})$ than that of MIL-53 $(2.7{\mathrm{V}})$ . This research group also explored the electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) results to discuss the alkali ion (lithium or sodium ion) transfer/diffusion at the electrode–electrolyte interface. Moreover, the ex situ XRD and XPS results suggested that the redox-active sites for K salt were Fe ions, while for Rb salt, the redoxactive sites were Mn ions during the entire charge–discharge process [71]. Later, bimetallic CuFe-PBA with the composition $\mathrm{K_{0.14}C u_{~1.43}^{H}[F e(C N)_{6}]}{\cdot5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ was synthesized through a precipitation method in an aqueous solution. Here, the authors studied the magnetism of the produced electrode when it was reversibly switched between ferromagnetism and paramagnetism due to the electrochemical Li-ion insertion/extraction reaction. This complete transformation from ferromagnetism to paramagnetism by guest ions is very rare and allowed 1.75 lithium ions per Fe atom to be inserted during the charge–discharge process. Furthermore, the authors concluded that lithium-ion insertion was accompanied by a reversible redox reaction of both Fe and $\mathrm{Cu}$ ions [44].
Wang et  al. prepared a nanocomposite by ball milling graphene oxide powder and nanoparticles of potassium manganese ferrocyanide (ratios of $12\%$ C and $88\%$ $\mathrm{K_{1.8}M n_{1.1}F e(C N)_{6}.0.27H_{2}O)}$ with a subsequent thermal treatment at $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under vacuum in which PBA nanoparticles were evenly distributed between graphene layers [73]. Both potassium manganese ferrocyanide (MPB) and potassium manganese ferrocyanide dispersed in rGO (MPB/rGO) were subjected to an electrochemical performance investigation. As expected, MPB/rGO exhibited enhanced electrochemical performance compared to pure MPB owing to the support received by the MPB nanoparticles from the rGO nanosheets through the 3D matrix, which was favorable for promoting good mechanical stability and enhanced electron transport properties. The nanocomposite exhibited enhanced electrochemical performance with a specific capacity of $150\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ at an average of $3.8\mathrm{V}$ and remained more than $120\mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ for 35 cycles. This excellent electrochemical performance and good cyclability were distinctly higher than those of other reported Fe-based PBA compounds, and these results could also be considered competitive with standard cathode materials of Li-ion batteries. Ternary-metal Prussian blue analog nanoparticles as positive electrode materials for Li-ion batteries were investigated by Okubo et al. to test heterometal substitutions on electrochemical properties [74]. Initially, the authors synthesized $(\mathbf{Mn}_{0.5}\mathbf{Cu}_{0.5})\mathrm{Fe}.$ -PBA through a precipitation method by dropwise adding aqueous solutions of $\mathrm{MnCl}_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathrm{CuSO}_{4}{\cdot}5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ to a ${\mathrm{K}}_{3}[\mathrm{Fe(CN)}_{6}]$ aqueous solution. The proposed formula for the as-synthesized Prussian blue analog was $\mathrm{K}_{0.1}(\mathrm{Mn}_{0.5}\mathrm{Cu}_{0.5})[\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]_{0.7}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ in which the partial replacement of redox-active ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ (in CuFe-PBA) with redox-inactive Mn metal (in MnFe-PBA) achieved both enhanced rate capability and cycling stability when compared to CuFe-PBA. This result again proves that MOF coordination compounds obey a rational strategy that allows for programmable tunability of the structure and composition to obtain better electrochemical properties. Importantly, the authors clarified the origin of the improved cycling stability with the help of ex situ XRD measurements. In this case, Li-rich and Li-poor phase separation was observed, but this separation was hampered due to Mn heterometal substitution, which in turn facilitated the charge/discharge process of the prepared electrode entirely through the solid-solution process. Later, two Prussian blue materials, $\mathrm{Fe}_{4}[\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]_{3}$ and ${\mathrm{FeFe}}({\mathrm{CN}})_{6}$ , were constructed via a simple one-step coprecipitation reaction and were then tested as cathodes in Li-ion batteries [46]. Owing to its high crystallinity and large crystallite size, ${\mathrm{FeFe}}({\mathrm{CN}})_{6}$ showed superior cycling and rate performances (first discharge capacity of $138~\mathrm{{mAh}~g^{-1}}$ decreased to $96\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}$ after 50 cycles) when compared to $\mathrm{Fe}_{4}[\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]_{3}$ (first discharge capacity of $95~\mathrm{mAh~g^{-1}}$ decreased to approximately $75\%$ of its initial capacity after 50 cycles). Furthermore, the high specific capacity and good cyclability of ${\mathrm{FeFe}}({\mathrm{CN}})_{6}$ might be ascribed to the large open space in the framework structure that provided easy transport and storage of Li ions. Another wet-chemical coprecipitation method was used to synthesize a border-rich iron $({\mathrm{Fe}}^{3+})$ hexacyanocobaltate (FeHCCo) MOF that was studied as a cathode material for Li-ion batteries [75]. The facile room-temperature reaction with inexpensive precursors demonstrated the morphology of hierarchical cubic crystals with rough surfaces based on scanning electron microscopy (SEM), and C, N, Fe, Co and K were uniformly dispersed based on energy-dispersive spectroscopy (EDS); additionally, these cubic crystals had a size of approximately $200\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 6). The electrochemical profile showed that the main compound delivered a 10th cycle lithiation capacity of $121\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ at a $1.25\mathrm{C}$ rate, which was maintained at $116\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ after 100 cycles with 2nd cycle Coulombic efficiency of $92.4\%$ . This good cyclability, which was associated with the high Coulombic efficiency, was mainly attributed to the isotropic and negligible lattice strains during Li-ion intercalation (lattice expansion)/extraction (lattice shrinkage); thus, the structure accommodated the volume changes during cycling. Finally, the authors concluded that the method of synthesis could be easily scaled up and was viable for use in large-scale energy storage electric grids. From the data presented in Table 2, one can compare the electrochemical profiles of various Fe-based MOF cathodes.
## 3.2  Other Transition Metal‑Based MOFs as Cathode Materials
While focusing on MOF chemistry, there is a broad scope for generating a large number of novel MOF materials via rational design, ligand exchange, structural engineering, etc., and many research groups have resorted to the development of new MOF cathodes for reversible Li-ion storage in lithium-ion batteries. Owing to the noticeable energyrelated advantages, in this section, we review MOF cathodes other than iron-based MOF candidates in continuation of the above section. Initially, Nguyen et al. developed MOFs by employing the redox-active organic ligand tetrathiafulvalene tetracarboxylic acid $\mathrm{(TTF–TC)H_{4}}$ with divalent $\mathrm{\DeltaNi}$ and Co [76]. These two candidates were studied for both their magnetic and redox properties. It was observed that both exhibited weak magnetic coupling. Thereafter, regarding redox properties, the first charge capacity of both MIL-136(Ni) and MIL-136(Co) was ${\sim}20\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ at a rate of $10\mathrm{C}$ between 2.0 and $4.3\mathrm{~V~}$ due to the TTF core, and the redox reaction was found to be irreversible from the second oxidation onward. Thus, the performance of these materials as a cathode for Li batteries was poor in contrast to cathodes based on TTF linkers and alkaline ions. The coordination of alkali salts $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{M}}=\mathbf{\boldsymbol{K}}$ , Rb, Cs) with redox-active $\mathrm{(TTF–TC)H_{4}}$ linkers led to the formation of 3D coordination polymers MIL132(K), MIL-133 (isostructural K, Rb) and MIL-134(Cs), respectively. As an efficient positive electrode for lithiumion batteries, all three candidates exhibited similar performance [77]. Henceforth, the authors stated that MIL-136(Ni) and MIL-136(Co) complexes were not suitable as cathodes for Li batteries; however, a fully reversible two-electron oxidation process was observed due to the redox-active organic linker moiety. A microporous vanadium-based MOF with the composition ${\mathrm{V}}^{\mathrm{IV}}({\mathrm{O}})({\mathrm{bdc}})$ , [MIL-47] was also reported [47]. This polycrystalline structure was isostructural to the previously reported MIL-53(Fe) MOF and was later studied for its electrochemical reaction as a cathode in Li cells. Upon reversible cycling, this cathode delivered a capacity of $82{\mathrm{~mAh~g^{-1}}}$ at a current density of $C/12$ with $\sim100\%$ Coulombic efficiency. The capacity of MIL-47 was higher than that of isostructural MIL-53(Fe), since one mole of ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ per formula unit $\mathrm{[Li}_{x}\mathrm{V(O)}$ (bdc), $x=1]$ could be reversibly inserted corresponding to the $\mathrm{V}^{4+}/\mathrm{V}^{3+}$ redox couple during Li-ion intercalation/deintercalation. The good rate capability and capacity of MIL-47 could be ascribed to its open framework structure with empty channels having a small particle size of $\sim80{-}300\ \mathrm{nm}$ facilitating a sufficient contact area between the active cathode material and electrolyte, while providing short diffusion paths for ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ . Tian et al. designed a series of naphthalenediimide-based MOFs via the complexation of an ${\bf N},{\bf N^{\prime}}$ -di(4-pyridyl)-1,4,5,8-naphthalenediimide (DPNDI) ligand and $\mathrm{Cd}\mathrm{X}_{2}$ $\mathrm{(X=NO_{3}}^{-}$ or $\mathrm{ClO}_{4}^{-}$ ) to produce MOFs 1 and 2 $\mathbf{\dot{Cd}}(\mathrm{II})\mathbf{MOFs})$ , as shown in Fig. 7 [78]. Similarly, the complexation of DPNDI with $\mathrm{Co}(\mathsf{N C S})_{2}$ produced MOFs 3 and 4 $(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{II})\mathrm{MOFs})$ as kinetic and thermodynamic products. The structures of MOFs 1 to 4 were confirmed by using direct methods and later refined by full-matrix least squares methods on F2 using SHELXL-2014, a crystal structure refinement program.
When tested as cathode materials for structure-dependent electrochemical performance in lithium-ion batteries, MOFs 1 and 2 showed initial specific capacities of 42.1 and $46.8~\mathrm{mAh}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $100\ \mathrm{mA\g^{-1}}$ , respectively, with capacity retention ratios of $77.2\%$ and $78.4\%$ after 50 cycles, respectively. Similarly, MOFs 3 and 4 delivered specific capacities of 45.9 and $62.7\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ at $100\mathrm{mAg^{-1}}$ , respectively. All four MOFs exhibited good electrochemical stability and reversibility owing to the strong influence of their metal nodes. The initial capacities of MOFs 3 and 4 $(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{II})$ MOFs) were higher than that of $\mathrm{Cd(II)MOFs}$ ; however, the cycling performance was poor for MOFs 3 and 4 owing to their structural instability during cycling. In addition to metal nodes, the difference in the electrochemical performance was mainly ascribed to stability and high porosity, in which MOF 2 possesses high porosity and allowed much more lithium-ion diffusion than that observed with the other
MOFs. Inspired by the notable cathodic performance of layered $\mathbf{LiCoO}_{2}$ metal oxides, Du et al. designed and reported a solvothermal synthesis strategy to synthesize two ternary Li–Co–MOFs named SNNU-73 and SNNU-74. The naming of these MOFs was based on the authors home institution, i.e., SNNU stands for “Shaanxi Normal University”, while SNNU-73 and SNNU-74 reflect the location of lithium ions at the same or different sites as cobalt atoms, respectively, and the four-coordinated tetrahedron mode was used to coordinate with oxygen atoms [79]. This structure provided a unique opportunity to investigate the synergistic effect of mixed lithium and cobalt sites on the electrochemical performance of SNNU-73 and SNNU-74. When studied for their electrochemical properties as cathodes in lithium-ion batteries, both MOFs provided a good platform for the insertion and extraction of lithium ions owing to the 3D porous framework structure. This channel-type MOF with a helix chain structure (SNNU-73) delivered a first discharge capacity of $155.6\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ at $50\mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and exhibited a stable capacity of 67.5 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ over 50 cycles. Conversely, the cagebased MOF (SNNU-74) delivered a first discharge capacity of $87.9~\mathrm{mAh}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $50\ \mathrm{mA\g^{-1}}$ and exhibited a lower capacity $(\sim40\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}},$ ) than SNNU-73 over 50 cycles. In addition, the first cycle Coulombic efficiency of these two MOF cathodes was more than $100\%$ , which was attributed to the additional lithium-ion extraction from the stable solid electrolyte interface (SEI) film.
To make use of the advantages of redox-active sites present in organic materials and metal clusters, Awaga et al. were the first to report a redox copper-based MOF for utilization as a cathode in lithium-ion batteries [80]. A facile solvothermal reaction between copper metal nodes and the redox-active organic bridging ligand, 2,7-anthraquinonedicarboxylic acid $(2,7\mathrm{-}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{AQDC}$ , which exhibits two reversible redox steps with an uptake of one electron for each step), led to the formation of a 2D MOF with a Kagome lattice architecture. The formula for $\mathrm{Cu}(2,7-$ AQDC) was found to be $\{\mathbf{Cu}(2,7\mathbf{-AQDC})(\mathbf{DMF})\}_{\infty}{\cdot}x\mathbf{DMF}$ , since both the copper metal and anthraquinone ligand had independent redox-active sites and both underwent a reduction process, as per the in situ $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray absorption near edge structure (XANES) and cyclic voltammetry results. An initial specific capacity of $147\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}$ was observed, which was close to the theoretical capacity $(\sim162\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1},$ ). This value was also comparable to the theoretical specific capacity of the major commercial cathode material, $\mathrm{LiMn}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ $(148\mathrm{~mAh~g^{-1})}$ . Although the initial capacity was very close to the theoretical value, the battery suffered from a capacity loss of approximately $40\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ before stabilizing at $\sim105\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ within 50 cycles. This capacity loss might be attributed to the inadequate extraction of lithium ions during the charge process. Finally, the authors concluded that the design of a novel MOF cathode material with independent redox-active sites on both the organic material and the transition metal cluster would achieve larger surface areas and higher reduction voltages for high-performance lithium-ion batteries. Another redoxactive ligand, tricarboxytriphenyl amine $\mathrm{(H}_{3}\mathrm{TCA)}$ , was used by Peng et al. to rationally combine with a redoxactive copper metal cluster to construct a triphenylaminebased MOF material, $\mathrm{Cu}$ -TCA, for use as a cathode in lithium-ion batteries [81]. The performance of $\mathrm{Cu}$ -TCA was comparable to that of the commercial $\mathbf{LiCoO}_{2}$ cathode material, and exhibited redox activity contributions from both the metal nodes $(\mathrm{Cu}^{+}/\mathrm{Cu}^{2+})$ and organic ligand radicals $(\mathrm{N}/\mathrm{N}^{+})$ with separate voltage plateaus (demonstrating two redox regions) and a high working potential of up to $4.3\mathrm{~V~}$ vs. $\mathrm{Li/Li^{+}}$ . The $\mathrm{Cu}$ -TCA electrode was electrochemically activated by a predischarge process at a $0.5~\mathrm{C}$ rate, and the first charge capacity was $139.1\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}$ at a cutoff potential of $4.3\mathrm{~V~}$ , which was close to its theoretical capacity. The cycling stability of the Cu-TCA electrode was enhanced by using an electrolyte additive, hexamethylene diisocyanate (HDI). Generally, this electrolyte additive has been used for high-voltage cathode materials, such as $\mathrm{LiNi_{1/3}C o_{1/3}M n_{1/3}O_{2}}$ , to improve their performance through the formation of protective layers on their surface. By adding this additive, an initial high discharge capacity of
$63.1\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ at a $2\mathrm{C}$ rate was delivered (the theoretical capacity was $145\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}},$ , and the capacity retention was $71.5\%$ $45.1\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1})}$ after 200 cycles with average Coulombic efficiency of $96.5\%$ . The capacity loss was attributed to the loss of redox activity on the $\mathtt{C u}$ ions and the structural instability of the framework. The first report of a phthalocyanine-based 2D MOF $\left(\mathrm{Cu-CuPc}\right)$ was demonstrated by Nagatomi et al. via the complexation of copper(II) 2,3,9,10,16,17,23,24-octahydroxy-29H,31Hphthalocyanine $({\mathrm{CuPcOH}})$ with copper $(\mathrm{II})$ ions [82]. This black two-dimensional (2D) MOF powder was developed by the square-planar style bridging of tetracatechol copper(II) phthalocyanine with copper(II) ions. The selection of phthalocyanine as a planar bridging unit was due to its good redox activity and carrier transport capability stemming from its large delocalized $\pi$ system. Similarly, catechol derivatives have the ability to generate square-planar complexes with transition metal ions, which in turn exert redox activity for reversible lithium-ion storage. The LIB cathode material (theoretical capacity of $304.3\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}},$ ) delivered an initial large charge/discharge capacity of $151/128\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}$ at a $0.04\mathrm{~C~}$ rate, which was $49\%$ of the theoretical capacity. When studied for its cycling stability, the cathode material reached its highest capacity of $55\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at approximately 70th cycle at a $0.4\mathrm{C}$ rate and remained stable until the 200th cycle with Coulombic efficiency of $595\%$ . This promising LIB cathodic performance mainly originated from the 2D hierarchical micro/mesoporous structure (which prevented structural deterioration during lithium-ion insertion) with the concurrent increase in electrical conductivity and redox activity of the phthalocyanine-based framework structure.
The exploration of novel cathode candidates for highrate lithium-ion batteries is a continuously challenging task to achieve future energy demands, and with this aim, Gu et al. synthesized the conductive MOF $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})_{2}$ with the formula $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(2,3,6,7,10,11\$ -hexahydroxytriphenylene) $\vert_{2}$ [83]. Here, the authors anticipated that both the parallellayered structure along the $c$ -axis and the weak interaction between the layers would facilitate the smooth intercalation of lithium ions at a level several orders of magnitude higher than other materials. The highly intrinsic electrical conductivity (because of the extended 2D $\pi$ -conjugation) and the open porous layered framework provided unrestricted tunnel paths for Li-ion intercalation/deintercalation. The cathode material, a MOF $(80\%)$ crystalline powder mixed with the polyvinylidene fluoride (PVDF, $20\mathrm{wt}\%$ ) binder in N-methyl pyrrolidone without the use of any conductive carbon additives, exhibited a good discharge capacity of $\sim105\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ upon its first lithiation and steadily maintained a value of $\sim95\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ between 20 and 60 cycles. Afterward, there was a capacity decay of $8.1\%$ between 60 and 100 cycles, while $\sim50\%$ capacity was retained over 500 cycles with a capacity decay of $0.09\%$ per cycle. The possibility of an unwanted reaction between lithium ions due to the layered structure and hydroxyl groups might be responsible for the capacity decay of the electrode. In 2020, Jiang et al. reported a new redox-active 2D copper benzoquinoid metal–organic framework (Cu-THQ MOF) endowed with high porosity and intrinsic redox characteristics; this material exhibited promising electrochemical activity as a cathode material for LIBs [84]. The 2D $\mathrm{Cu}$ -THQ MOF was synthesized through a simple and modified hydrothermal approach, which resulted in the formation of a 2D honeycomb-layered framework via a topological combination between the tetrahydroxy-1,4-quinone (THQ) linker and copper ions. The crystallite size was approximately $10{-}30~\mathrm{nm}$ with a BET surface area of $\sim137$ $\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and a pore volume of $0.43\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . Its superior electrochemical performance was achieved in the second cycle with a large reversible capacity of 387 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ (specific energy density of $775~\mathrm{Wh~kg^{-1}},$ at $50\ \mathrm{mA\g^{-1}}$ with $90\%$ CE. It was observed that the capacity of the above material was higher than that of other reported MOF cathode materials. Due to the high crystallinity of 2D $\mathrm{Cu}$ -THQ MOF, an exceptional cycling stability with a reversible capacity of $340\mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ after 100 cycles ( $85\%$ capacity retention) was achieved, as shown in Fig. 8. The authors finally demonstrated that both copper ions and the THQ linker contributed notable redox activity during the charge/discharge process and elucidated the mechanism as a three-electron redox reaction per coordination unit and a one-electron redox reaction per copper ion with the help of comprehensive spectroscopic techniques. Interestingly, a zirconium $(Z\mathbf{r})$ -based MOF UiO66 (UiO stands for University of Oslo) with the formula $(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4})_{4}(1,$ 4-benzenedicarboxylate) $)_{6}$ , where $Z\mathrm{r}$ is a redox-inactive transition metal, was reported and investigated as a positive electrode material for LIBs [85]. Usually, most research groups focus on the redox activity of metal nodes in MOFs to store charge in batteries, but in this case, the study relied on anions for the electrochemical reactions. The authors discussed the origins (aromatic ring, carboxylate oxygen, oxygen node and replaced hydrogen node) for charge storage in the UiO-66 system and presented the quantum mechanical characterization of the mechanism as well as experimental studies. The above material was synthesized as nanoparticles by lowering the reaction temperature to reduce diffusion paths for lithium ions and to improve the contact area with the conductive carbon phase. The band gap of UiO-66 greatly decreased during the lithiation process, which was anticipated to improve its electrical conductivity. The calculated changes in the IR spectra of UiO-66 and lithiated UiO-66 matched the experimental FTIR results. Furthermore, UiO-66 delivered a specific discharge capacity of $118\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ for 30 lithiation and delithiation cycles at C/5, with good cyclability.
Asakura et al. extended their efforts and produced host frameworks, i.e., cyanide-bridged PB analogs, as cathode electrodes for lithium-ion batteries with enhanced ion storage ability [36]. Electrochemical guest-ion storage via an ion insertion/extraction mechanism in a host framework associated with a solid-state redox reaction is an important fundamental issue in battery technology. Ion storage requires the host framework to possess redox-active sites and porous space to store electrons as well as ions, respectively. Keeping this in mind, the authors initially synthesized ${\bf K}_{2}{\bf M}{\bf n}^{\mathrm{II}}[{\bf M}{\bf n}^{\mathrm{II}}({\bf C}{\bf N})_{6}]$ via a precipitation method and later suppressed the framework electrochemically to form vacancies by the complete extraction of all K ions accompanied by the oxidation of both $\mathrm{{Mn}_{C}}$ and $\mathrm{{\mathbf{M}n_{\mathrm{N}}}}$ . Notably, the resulting twoelectron redox behavior of this material was different from the redox behavior of conventional PBAs. Figure $9\mathrm{c}$ shows the open-circuit voltages (OCVs) during Li-ion insertion/ extraction obtained by the galvanostatic intermittent titration technique (GITT) and the discharge–charge curves at a constant current density of $30\mathrm{\mA\g^{-1}}$ , and Fig. 9e represents the structural changes and redox reactions of the prepared compound framework during Li-ion insertion/ extraction. The vacancy-free PBA framework, $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{{III}}}[\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{{III}}}$ $(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]_{\ 0.93}[\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]_{0.07}{\cdot}0.65\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , was studied for its cathodic performance, and it was found from the results that the 1st discharge capacity of $197\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}$ corresponded to $1.91\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ insertion with a Coulombic efficiency reaching almost $100\%$ (Fig. 9d). This value was considerably higher than an earlier report $(60\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\cdot$ ) [70], but the cyclability of the vacancy-suppressed PBA was poor, which decreased gradually after a few cycles, thus affecting its practical application in batteries. However, this enhanced storage of lithium ions opened new prospects in possible electrode material designs for rechargeable batteries. Recently, octacyanometallate-bridged coordination polymers have both topological flexibility and enhanced magnetic exchange interactions due to their high coordination number and the diffusion of the $4\mathrm{d}/5\mathrm{d}$ orbital, respectively, so that the structural and magnetic diversity of cyano-bridged coordination polymers has also been extended. A octacyanometallate-bridged coordination polymer with the formula $[\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})][\mathrm{Mn}-$ $(\mathrm{HCOO})_{2/3}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2/3}]_{3/4}[\mathrm{Mo}(\mathrm{CN})_{8}]{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , which is analogous to PBAs, was synthesized by a precipitation method and investigated for its solid-state electrochemical Li-ion insertion/ extraction reaction [86]. The compound had a structurally analyzed double-decked structure with a 2D ionic diffusion path, and the ion insertion/extraction process was associated with the switching of its magnetic properties, reversibly transforming between ferrimagnetism and paramagnetism. A reversible specific capacity of $30\mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ was obtained at a specific current of $10\ \mathrm{mA\g^{-1}}$ applied between cutoff voltages of 3.0 and $4.3{\mathrm{~V}}.$ In this case, $0.53\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ ions were reversibly inserted and extracted, which remained constant even after 100 insertion/extraction cycles. The high durability of the electrochemical response was attributed to the cyano-bridged networks with octacyanometallate building blocks, promoting its mixed conductivity and small lattice change (less than $1\%$ ).
In addition to the MOF cathodes discussed above, a novel type of phosphate-based hybrid material called metal–organic phosphate open frameworks (MOPOFs), which have excellent structural diversity, have also been studied and reported as cathodes for Li-ion batteries. The application of MOF candidates as electrodes for lithium-ion batteries faces some challenges, such as their low density and high molecular weights, large-scale synthesis obstacles and the difficulty of robust MOFs to isolate with redoxactive centers [87]. These drawbacks impede the commercialization of MOF cathode materials, and hence, some research groups have investigated this new type of phosphate-based hybrid material for energy storage applications. MOPOFs are multidimensional hybrid materials constructed from transition metal phosphates cross-linked by simple organic linkers that in turn possess the ability to encapsulate alkali ions, for example, ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ , $\mathrm{\DeltaNa^{+}}$ and $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ , between layers. Enriched with a variety of structural properties, a plethora of MOPOFs is available in the literature for conventional industrial applications, such as catalysis, gas storage, redox reactions and MRI contrast agents; however, there are only a few reports of MOPOFs used as energy storage materials in LIBs [88–90].
In their reports, the authors made use of the presence of mixed organic oxalate and inorganic phosphate anions (which improved the redox properties of the transition metal ions), in addition to the versatility offered by organic linkers to achieve a robust material that could be successfully applied as cathode materials in lithium-ion batteries. For comparison, their electrochemical performances are presented along with the electrochemical profiles of other MOFs in Table 3. The suitable low-temperature synthesis conditions and the combination of organic oxalate and inorganic phosphate anions with redox-active transition metal ions make them good energy storage materials. Nagarathinam et al. were the first to report the synthesis of three MOPOFs, $\mathrm{K}_{2.5}[(\mathrm{VO})_{2}(\mathrm{HPO}_{4})_{1.5}(\mathrm{PO}_{4})_{0.5}(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4})]$ , 1 , $\mathbf{Na}_{2}[(\mathbf{VO})_{2}(\mathbf{HPO}_{4})_{2}(\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{4})]$ , 2 , a n d K L i $[(\mathrm{VO})_{2}(\mathrm{HPO}_{4})_{2}(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4})].$ , 3, on a gram scale and for the first time investigated their lithium storage performance as cathodes in LIBs [91]. The feasibility of the extraction of $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ and the subsequent insertion of ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ in the first and subsequent cycles was illustrated by the isolation and characterization of the lithiated species 3. Similarly, a systematic study of the electrochemical performance was performed with 1, which disclosed the facile extraction and insertion of alkali metal ions. Cathode materials 2 and 3 showed comparable voltage profiles at the end of the 25th cycle with discharge capacities of 55 and $60\mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ , respectively, and cathode material 1 showed a discharge capacity of $59\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ (the first cycle charge capacity was $85\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1},$ ) at the end of the 60th cycle. Apart from this, the electrochemical characteristics of the samples remained unaltered upon storage for 8 months under ambient conditions. This study was concluded with the feasibility of tailoring the system in regard to the voltage and near-theoretical capacity by changing the alkali metals, redox-active transition metal centers, simple organic linkers and polyanion moieties with better, less expensive materials with new architectures and provided a route for the use of these materials as substitutes for MOF cathode materials in commercial Li-ion cells. Later, the group developed two more cathode materials, $\mathrm{Li}_{2}(\mathrm{VO})_{2}(\mathrm{HPO}_{4})_{2}(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4})$ and $\mathrm{rGO/K}_{2}[(\mathrm{VO})_{2}(\mathrm{HPO}_{4})_{2}(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4})]$ , and studied their potential as cathode materials in LIBs [48, 92]. The synthesis of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}(\mathrm{VO})_{2}(\mathrm{HPO}_{4})_{2}(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4})$ was carried out through a simple hydrothermal method with simultaneous ab initio structure determination using high-quality powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) data. This Li-bearing hybrid inorganic–organic open framework structure exhibited a first cycle charge capacity of $75~\mathrm{mAh~g^{-1}}$ at a current rate of $0.1\mathrm{{C}}$ , which stabilized after 10 cycles and retained charge and discharge capacities of 83 and $80\mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ at the end of 25 cycles, respectively, with $97\%$ Coulombic efficiency. The layered structure of the above material allowed the presence of lithium ions in the interlayer space through insertion and reinsertion, and the simultaneous redox couple of $\mathrm{V}^{4+/5+}$ made this lithiumcontaining oxalatophosphate material worthy of cathode applications in lithium-ion batteries. Later, $\mathrm{rGO}/\mathrm{K}_{2}[(\mathrm{VO})_{2}(\$ $\mathrm{HPO}_{4})_{2}(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4})]$ was synthesized at room temperature in aqueous solution using environmentally abundant tartaric acid as an organic source, which was cost-effective and eliminated costly postsynthetic treatments in an inert atmosphere. The in situ breakdown of tartaric acid to oxalate triggered the formation of an oxalatophosphate framework and exhibited discharge capacities of 98 and $103\mathrm{~mAh~g^{-1}}$ over 50 cycles for cathodes with 4 and $8\mathrm{wt}\%$ rGO, respectively, in lithiumion batteries. In this case, rGO provided increased electrical conductivity and enhanced lithium diffusivity because a pristine MOPOF exhibits capacity fading, despite the material exhibiting highly reversible lithium storage. Conversely, rGO/MOPOF showed enhanced lithium cycling, which was associated with minimal capacity fading.
## 4  MOF‑Derived Cathode Materials for LIBs
The surge of lithium-ion battery technology has encountered many critical issues, such as safety, cost, targeted energy density demands and environmental concerns. After their commercialization in the 1990s, most of them surpassed the eminent efforts of many research groups, and now, they have become the dominant technology to power hybrid electric vehicles and portable systems. As an important battery component, several MOF cathodes have been developed and tested in addition to the traditional cathode materials in LIBs to achieve better battery performance. To the best of our knowledge, we have discussed almost all MOF candidates in the above sections that warrant study as cathode candidates for lithium-ion batteries. However, the reported MOF cathodes struggle with inferior rate performance, dampening of the framework structure during cycling, electrolyte decomposition, thermostability and so on. In fact, due to the low conductivity of MOFs, practical electrochemical applications are difficult. Several approaches have been adapted to address these issues, and one of the paramount approaches is an MOF template-mediated synthesis to overcome these shortcomings through the generation of a wide variety of MOF-derived nanostructures [28]. The versatility of postsynthetic modifications that involve the conversion of pristine MOFs to their derived nanostructures for cathode applications in lithium-ion batteries is discussed in Sect. 2. In addition, the in situ growth and/or coating of MOFs over the surface of commercial cathode candidates to enhance their electrochemical properties are also discussed. Fortunately, the discovery of MOF-derived cathode materials can offer full access for engineering their structural and morphological features, which in turn greatly address bottlenecks (complex synthesis protocols, limited energy density and/or short cycling life) encountered by traditional cathode materials. One successful approach is the surface modification of high-performance commercial cathode materials with MOFs or MOF-derived materials, which improves electrode stability during cycling. The second approach is the derivation of metal component nanostructures and porous carbonaceous materials from pristine MOFs obtained through controlled thermal treatments. The as-obtained metal component nanostructures and porous carbonaceous materials can be directly utilized as cathodes in lithium-ion battery applications.
## 4.1  MOF‑Modified Commercial Cathode Candidates
Wang et al. reported a mechanochemical synthesis method for the preparation of $\mathbf{LiCoO}_{2}$ (LCO) followed by surface coatings with two distinct UiO-66 and MIL-53 MOFs and their calcination products $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{Z}r O}_{2}$ and ${\mathrm{Al}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ , respectively [93]. To improve the electrochemical properties of $\mathrm{LiCoO}_{2}$ , commonly used metal oxide coating materials, such as $\mathbf{{ZrO}}_{2}$ and ${\mathrm{Al}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ , were synthesized via the MOF template synthesis route and achieved a uniform coating over LCO, as shown in Fig. 10a. When studied for their cathodic performance in LIBs at a current density of $2325~\mathrm{mA~g^{-1}}$ , a slightly lower initial capacity was observed for UiO- $66@\mathrm{LiCoO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}@\mathrm{LiCoO}_{2}$ , $\mathbf{MIL-}53@\mathbf{LiCoO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{LiCoO}_{2}$ than for bare $\mathbf{LiCoO}_{2}$ . Conversely, outstanding cycling performance was observed for UiO- $66@\mathrm{LiCoO}_{2}$ $(89.5\%)$ , $\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}@$ $\mathbf{LiCoO}_{2}$ $(71.1\%)$ , MIL- ${53@\mathrm{LiCoO}_{2}}$ $(79.6\%)$ and $\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@$ $\mathrm{LiCoO}_{2}$ $(72.8\%)$ compared with bare $\mathrm{LiCoO}_{2}$ $(12.9\%)$ after 100 cycles at $2325~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . The surface-modified $\mathrm{LiCoO}_{2}$ showed impressive rate performance, and the MOF-derived protective layers could effectively alleviate the structural changes of $\mathbf{LiCoO}_{2}$ during cycling even at elevated temperature $(55^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ . Makiura et al. reported the surface modification of PB nanolayers on LCO particle surfaces $({\mathrm{PBNL@LCO}})$ via a solution-based sequential growth technique [49]. The deposition of lithium cobalt oxide was started with isonicotinic acid (acting as an adhesive layer), in which the carboxylic groups were attached to the LCO surface and the pyridyl parts allowed the subsequent introduction of iron ions via coordinative bonding. The alternating immersion of $\mathbf{LiCoO}_{2}$ into solutions of $\mathrm{FeCl}_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and ${\mathrm{K}}_{3}[\mathrm{Fe(CN)}_{6}]$ resulted in the generation of finely controlled immersion cycles to obtain PBN $\boldsymbol{\mathscr{Q}}\mathrm{LCO}$ . The LIB cathodic performance was too low for three cycles of PBNL-deposited LCO $\mathrm{(100~mAh~g^{-1}}$ ), but the LCO particles with one cycle of PBNL deposition delivered a discharge capacity of $130\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ , which was lower than the specific capacity of LCO $(\sim150\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1},$ .
Wang et al. reported another material with an MOFderived alumina (MDA) coating on the surface of the widely employed cathode material $\mathrm{LiNi_{0.6}C o_{0.2}M n_{0.2}O_{2}}$ (NCM622), as shown in Fig. 11 [37]. The authors selected NCM622 for its excellent high capacity that benefitted from its major content of Ni at transition metal sites; even so, few problems may hinder the commercialization of this cathode material. The high content of Ni caused reduced structural stability and drove the material to experience the loss of lattice oxygen when charged to $\geqslant4.5\mathrm{~V~}$ vs $\mathrm{Li^{+}/L i}$ , along with several electrolyte-driven side reactions. This behavior significantly led to a rise in surface impedance, diminished discharge capacity and inferior cycling stability [94].
To solve these problems, an $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-53 MOF coating precursor was first synthesized through a solvothermal approach, and NCM-622 was synthesized via a coprecipitation method. Later, the MOF and NCM-622 were ball-milled at a specific mass ratio, and the MOF-modified NCM-622 was calcined at $600^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in air to obtain MDA- $2.5@\mathrm{NCM}$ . Thanks to MOF-derived alumina (MDA) because, a $2.5\%$ MDA coating was well dispersed on NCM-622 with an amorphous structure showing enhanced initial discharge capacity of $196.5\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-}}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at a $\mathrm{~1~C~}$ rate (greater than NCM-622: $188.3\mathrm{~mAh~g^{-1})}$ with discharge capacity of 183.3 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ $92.7\%$ capacity retention) after 100 cycles (Fig. 11). This enhanced electrochemical performance was assigned to the surface protection of NCM-622, which hampered side reactions, and the well-dispersed amorphous nature that stabilized the crystalline structure of NCM-622 and consequently provided an abundance of pathways for ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ .
Li-rich layered $\mathrm{Li(Li_{0.17}N i_{0.20}C o_{0.05}M n_{0.58})O_{2}}$ oxide was initially synthesized via spray-drying and a solid-state reaction by Qiao et al. and later subjected to surface modifications with Mn-dhbdc MOF (dhbdc $^{=2,5}$ -dihydroxyterephthalic acid) to warrant it as a new cathode material for LIB systems [95]. The large discharge capacity $(250\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}},$ ) and excellent stability of this material at a high operating potential, make these Li-rich layered oxides as outstanding cathode candidates for advanced lithium-ion batteries. Both the as-prepared and MOF-modified samples were investigated for their electrochemical performance, and the MOF-modified sample delivered remarkably improved performance owing to the contribution received from its surface modification with a Mn-based MOF. A large discharge capacity of $323.8\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ at a $0.1\mathrm{{C}}$ rate was obtained for the MOF-modified sample with a high capacity retention of
$284.1\ \mathrm{mAh}$ $\mathrm{g}^{-1}(87.7\%)$ after 100 cycles with good thermal stability, cycling stability and high-rate capability. However, the as-prepared sample without MOF modification delivered an initial discharge capacity of approximately $271.4\mathrm{~mAh~g^{-1}}$ and retained $236.1\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ after 100 cycles. Furthermore, the improvement in electrochemical performance of the prepared electrode was mainly attributed to the good oxygen storage capability of the MOF layer. Similarly, Li-rich layered $\mathrm{Li_{1.2}M n_{0.54}N i_{0.13}C o_{0.13}O_{2}}$ oxide (LLO) nanoparticles were subjected to surface modification with MOFs through low-pressure vapor superassembly and then in situ converted into NiCo nanodot-decorated carbon shells under controlled pyrolysis to finally obtain NiCo nanodot-decorated (diameter of approximately $5\mathrm{nm}$ ) carbon-confined $\mathrm{Li}_{1.2}\mathrm{Mn}_{0.54}\mathrm{Ni}_{0.13}\mathrm{Co}_{0.13}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ nanoparticles simplified as LLO $@$ C&NiCo [38]. This LLO $@$ C&NiCo cathode exhibited improved cycling and rate capability ( $95\%$ capacity retention after 100 cycles at $0.4\mathrm{C}$ and a high capacity of $159\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $5\mathrm{C}$ ), which was relatively higher than that of the bare LLO. The enhanced performance of the LLO $@$ C&NiCo cathode was mainly assigned to the unique C&NiCo shell, which provided high electrical conductivity to the inner LLO nanoparticles and a robust structure. In another approach, Yim et al. reported the design and synthesis of MOFs, i.e., Co-MOF-74, as an advanced electrode additive for Ni-rich NCM cathodes to inhibit the dissolution of the transition metal components in a cell [96]. The electrochemical process of the Ni-rich NCM cathode involved electrolyte decomposition, which was accelerated due to the occurrence of unstable $\mathrm{Ni^{4+}}$ species, and in turn, generated nucleophilic fluoride $(\mathrm{F^{-}})$ species in the cell. These fluoride species trapped the unstable and electrophilic transition metal (TM) components via chemical reactions on the surface of the Ni-rich NCM cathode, which resulted in the TM components dissolving into the electrolyte. Notably, this reaction was not reversible, and the reversible redox reaction sites deformed permanently in the Ni-rich NCM cathode structure; this is undesirable behavior for their practical application [97]. To minimize this behavior, the authors developed a Co-MOF-74 additive that provided excellent performance for overcoming Ni dissolution by employing Ni-rich NCM cathodes in the cell. Regardless of the MOF addition, the first cycle specific capacity was almost identical; however, the cycling behavior was dependent on the amount of MOF additive. The NCM811 cathode with a $5\mathrm{wt}\%$ MOF (Co-MOF-74) additive showed no compromise in its electrochemical performance (first cycle capacity of $\sim205\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}},$ , which retained a remarkable $82.2\%$ of its first cycle capacity at high temperatures $(60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ after 100 cycles.
MOF-assisted treatment was developed for the surface modification of Li-rich layered oxide (LLO) material using a $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based MOF (UIO-66-F4 having the formula $Z\mathrm{r}$ -TFBDC) precursor, which on subsequent calcination produced MOF-derived $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{Z}r O}_{2}$ (MDZ) coatings [98]. Pristine $\mathrm{Li_{1.2}M n_{0.54}C o_{0.13}N i_{0.13}O_{2}}$ (LLO) microspheres were initially synthesized through a facile microwave-assisted hydrothermal method and later subjected to the in  situ generation of $\mathrm{Zr}$ -TFBDC-modified LLO microspheres using $\mathrm{Zr}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{4}$ and 2,3,5,6-tetrafluoro-1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid (TFBDC). Finally, MDZ $@$ LLO was obtained after calcining $\mathrm{Zr}$ -TFBDC-modified LLO microspheres for $6\mathrm{~h~}$ at $600^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in the presence of air. The $\mathrm{H^{+}{-}L i^{+}}$ exchange on the surface of the LLO particles was induced by the strong acidic character of TFBDC, which produced a uniform MOF coating with a porous structure on the LLO microspheres. The as-prepared MDZ@LLO cathode material exhibited first Coulombic efficiency of $72\%$ (CE of LLO is $62\%$ ) with an initial discharge capacity of $279.8\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ at a $1\mathrm{C}$ rate. The increase in the initial CE was attributed to F doping, which decreased the cell impedance for ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ diffusion and suppressed oxygen vacancy formation. After 200 cycles, the discharge capacity reached $171.3\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ with capacity retention above $83.2\%$ . Similarly, $\mathrm{{Xu}}$ et al. reported the surface modification of commercial ${\mathrm{LiFePO}}_{4}$ (LFP) with zeolitic imidazolate framework-8 (ZIF-8) to obtain LFP/ $\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{ZIF-8}}$ , as shown in Fig. 12 [99]. The in situ growth and carbonization of ZIF-8 offered commercial LFP with a notable electrochemical performance. According to the TEM results, the coating of carbonized ZIF-8 $(\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{ZIF-8}})$ on the surface of LFP was approximately $10\mathrm{nm}$ coating layer with zinc metal and graphite-like carbon. Similarly, the coating layer of the $\mathrm{LFP/C_{ZIF-8}}$ sample exhibited uniform and simply connected mesopores according to $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ adsorption and desorption isotherm results. This cathode-active material delivered discharge specific energy of $141.7\ \mathrm{mWh\g^{-1}}$ even after 200 cycles at a $5.0\mathrm{C}$ rate with a retention rate of approximately $99\%$ . The authors demonstrated that the degree of freedom for the volume change and the synergistic interaction of LFP with the carbonaceous material enhanced the cathodic performance of LFP/CZIF-8.
For the first time, molecular rearrangements in a highly stable and porous Ni-rich NCM using a Co-embedded metal–organic framework, ZIF-67, were reported by Park et  al. through mixing in a Henschel mixer followed by heat treatment. Initially, cobalt-containing ZIF-67 was introduced into Ni-rich NCM $(\mathrm{LiNi}_{0.80}\mathrm{Co}_{0.15}\mathrm{Mn}_{0.05}\mathrm{O}_{2})$ as a novel surface-modifying agent, which on subsequent thermal treatment formed a molecular-level thin layer of $\mathbf{CoO}_{x}$ species on the active material surface [53]. Residual
Co was incorporated into the layered Ni-rich NCM, followed by structural changes from a layered structure to a spinel-like phase structure (phase transformation), while the porous microstructure improved the cyclability and thermal stability of the material. Half-cells of $1\mathrm{wt}\%$ MOF-treated Ni-rich NCM cathodes showed a higher charge–discharge capacity of $210\mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ at a $0.1\mathrm{C}$ rate with a better cyclability of $95.1\%$ after 50 cycles than the untreated sample $(207\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ with a cyclability of $92.5\%$ ). The thermal stability also increased with an increasing MOF content and showed improved cyclability. A summary of the electrochemical performance of MOF-mediated surface-modified commercial cathodes in rechargeable lithium-ion batteries is presented in Table 4.
## 4.2  MOF‑Derived Metal Components as Cathodes
In addition to MOFs or MOF-derived material coatings on commercial cathodes, MOF-derived materials were also studied as cathode materials in Li-ion battery applications after postsynthesis modifications of pristine MOFs. The MOF-derived preparatory strategy avoids the particle aggregation problem, and nanoparticles of metallic compounds with special properties, such as metal oxides, metal phosphides, metal fluorides and metal sulfides, show promising benefits in various fields in addition to lithium storage. Furthermore, tuning electrochemical properties through the engineering of carbonaceous nanomaterials offers relief from severe morphological changes during cycling and provides high electrical conductivity (Table 5). Wang et al. reported the synthesis of C  NiS via the calcination of initially synthesized MOF-74(Ni) in a reduced environment, where nanosized Ni particles $(5-10~\mathrm{nm})$ were highly dispersed in carbon matrices [100]. The MOF template route is a promising strategy for the preparation of a variety of porous carbon materials that realize the removal of in situ generated metal nanoparticles, either by vaporization at high temperature or etching with an acid solution. Here, the authors followed the same strategy to synthesize metal nanoparticles that were highly dispersed in the porous carbon pool (C  NiS) without removing the in situ generated metal particles during calcination. The resulting material with small particle sizes, a homogeneous dispersion of metal nanoparticles and tunable porosity demonstrated the potential of this MOF template synthesis to realize useful materials as lithium-ion battery cathodes. Thiourea was used for sulfidation at two different temperatures, and the metal sulfide obtained at the low sulfidation temperature performed better (approximately $300\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ ) as an LIB cathode than the metal sulfide obtained at the high sulfidation temperature (approximately $200\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}.$ ) after 100 cycles at $60\mathrm{mAg^{-1}}$ . Both cathodes suffered severe capacity loss during the initial cycles due to the establishment of solid electrolyte interface (SEI) layers and the development of a stable charge–discharge system. The cathode reaction and the total charge–discharge mechanism of NiS are represented as follows:
$$
3\mathrm{NiS}+2\mathrm{Li}\leftrightarrow\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{2}+\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}
$$
$$
\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{2}+4\mathrm{Li}\leftrightarrow3\mathrm{Ni}+2\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}
$$
$$
\mathrm{NiS}+2\mathrm{Li}\leftrightarrow\mathrm{Ni}+\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}.
$$
Later, $\mathrm{Yu}$ et al. reported the facile top-down synthesis of a novel cathode material, namely, hollow cobalt sulfide nanoparticles (HCSP) embedded in graphitic carbon nanocages (GCC), $\mathrm{{HCSP}C G C C}$ , to reveal the power of microstructure engineering for enabling conversion reactions [54]. First, well-defined ZIF-67 rhombic dodecahedral nanocrystals were synthesized by the room-temperature precipitation method followed by low-temperature heat treatment, which realized pyrolysis and in situ reduction in a $\mathrm{\DeltaH_{2}/A r}$ atmosphere to obtain cobalt nanoparticles embedded in GCC $(\mathbf{CP}\mathsf{C}\mathbf{GCC})$ . Subsequently, a low-temperature solutionbased sulfidation of the ${\mathrm{CP}}{\mathsf{C G C C}}$ precursors was realized to obtain the prepared electrode material based on the Kirkendall effect. This distinct microstructure featured ultrasmall, hollow and continuous GCCs, resulting in high practical capacity and excellent rate capability during the charge/ discharge process through the effective prevention of the aggregation of electroactive nanoparticles. Due to its superior mechanical flexibility and pronounced structural stability, this LIB cathode exhibited outstanding energy density $(707~\mathrm{Wh~kg^{-1}})$ ), excellent reversible capacities of 536, 489, 438, 393, 345 and $278\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at 0.2, 0.5, 1, 2, 5 and $10\mathrm{C}$ , respectively, and stable cycling performance of $365\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}$ after 150 cycles at $1\mathrm{C}$ ( ${\approx}26\%$ capacity loss) at a working voltage of $1.0{-}3.0\ \mathrm{V}$ . The phase-controlled synthesis of $\mathtt{C u}_{x}\mathtt{S}$ composites within the graphitized carbon network via the temperature-controlled sulfurization of HKUST-1 was also reported [51]. As discussed, the MOF template approach is a simple method that involves the sulfurization of HKUST-1 for the tunable formation of different $\mathrm{Cu}_{x}\mathrm{S}$ phases within a carbon network. In this case, the authors demonstrated that a high sulfurization temperature favored the formation of more Cu-rich phases, and the $\mathrm{Cu}_{1.8}\mathrm{S}/\mathrm{C}{-}500$ nanowire composite phase exhibited the best performance and excellent long-term cycling stability as an LIB cathode material. This nanowire composite delivered a specific capacity of $220\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ with a cycling life of 200 cycles at $250\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ .
MIL-53, a Fe-based MOF, was used as a precursor/selftemplate to synthesize a graphitized 3D porous carbon/ $\mathrm{FeF}_{3}{\cdot}0.33\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (GCF) composite embedded in a carbon skeleton [101]. The authors stated that the highly graphitized carbon was derived from the organic ligands in iron-containing MOFs due to the catalysis of central Fe atoms. The composite exhibited excellent performance for Li storage because after fluorination and dehydration, the $\mathrm{Fe}\mathrm{F}_{3}{\cdot}0.33\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ nanoparticles were surrounded by highly graphitized 3D porous carbon, which provided a large number of passageways for electron transport while the ultrasmall $\mathrm{FeF}_{3}{\cdot}0.33\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ nanoparticles facilitated the diffusion of Li ions. However, the $_{\mathrm{O-FeF}_{3}\cdot0.33\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ cathode exhibited diminished performance compared with the GCF cathode (Table 5) [50]. The as-synthesized GCF cathode material performed well with an initial capacity of $129\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}$ at a $5\mathrm{~C~}$ rate and maintained capacity retention of $87\%$ $(\sim107\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ after 300 charge/discharge cycles. A hydrothermal process was used to prepare Fe-BTC-MOF, which was in turn subjected to a microwave-assisted solid-state approach along with nanosilica and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{Li}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ to obtain $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{FeSiO}_{4}/\mathrm{C}$ cathode composites [55]. The crystal sizes, morphology and electrochemical performances of these $\mathrm{Li_{2}F e S i O_{4}/C}$ composites were strongly influenced by the various carbon contents. These composites possessed the lowest charge transfer resistance and the highest ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ diffusion coefficient, which was attributed to their uniform carbon coating and the porous structure of the composite cathode material. The uniform carbon-coated composites with 19.3 $\mathbf{wt}\%$ carbon layers delivered a first discharge capacity of $117\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at a 2 C rate and retained a capacity of approximately $97.4\%$ after 100 cycles.
Porous carbon materials synthesized by using metal–organic frameworks as self-sacrificing templates have been increasingly studied due to their high conductivity and controllable characteristics. In particular, MOFs are increasingly utilized as raw materials to fabricate highlevel heteroatom-doped carbon materials. As one of the most investigated heteroatoms, nitrogen is notable owing to its higher electronegativity and smaller atomic diameter than those of carbon. Moreover, nitrogen atoms doped in carbon facilitate more active sites, leading to improved electrical conductivity and providing chemical bonds in carbon materials, which might be beneficial for preparing hybrid materials [102].
Lithium transition metal phosphates are another type of promising cathode material, among which $\mathrm{Li}_{3}\mathrm{V}_{2}(\mathrm{PO}_{4})_{3}$ is a potential candidate for lithium-ion battery applications for inexpensive commercial viability in energy applications. A facile soft chemistry route was demonstrated by Liang et al. to synthesize sandwich-like structured $\mathrm{Li}_{3}\mathrm{V}_{2}(\mathrm{PO}_{4})_{3}/\mathrm{Car}{-}$ bon (LVP/C) (Fig. 13a), in which ZIF-8-derived nitrogendoped carbon (with a diameter of $200{-}300~\mathrm{nm}$ ) formed an interconnected carbon matrix that improved the electrical conductivity of the composite during battery operation [103]. As an LIB cathode material, this composite exhibited a discharge specific capacity of $115~\mathrm{mAh}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ with a superior cycling stability, retaining $83.2\%$ of its capacity after 2500 cycles at a $5\mathrm{C}$ rate. To produce superior electrochemical performance, the MOF template synthesis route was adapted to synthesize $\mathrm{Li}_{3}\mathrm{V}_{2}(\mathrm{PO}_{4})_{3}$ by  using MIL-101(V) as the precursor because monoclinic $\mathrm{Li}_{3}\mathrm{V}_{2}(\mathrm{PO}_{4})_{3}$ itself suffers from an extremely low intrinsic electrical conductivity $(2.4\times10^{-7}\mathrm{S~cm^{-1}},$ ) [56]. The composition of this vanadium MOF was $\mathrm{[V_{3}O(b d c)_{3}(H_{2}O)_{2}C l_{0.7}(H b d c)_{0.3}]}\cdot2H_{2}O\cdot0.5\mathrm{EtOH}$ , which acted as a carbon and vanadium source for synthesizing carbon-coated $\mathrm{Li}_{3}\mathrm{V}_{2}(\mathrm{PO}_{4})_{3}$ nanocomposites denoted as $\mathbf{LVP}@\mathbf{M}-101$ . First, MIL-101(V) was synthesized, which is isostructural to Cr-MIL-101 and has a rigid structure with mesoporous cages; additionally, MIL-101(V) has the highest specific surface area among vanadium-based MOFs. Later, a two-step thermal decomposition of the MIL-101(V)-derived carbon-coated $\mathrm{Li}_{3}\mathrm{V}_{2}(\mathrm{PO}_{4})_{3}$ produced laminated structures with hollow bubbles and residual carbon both inside and outside. The electrochemical performance of the as-synthesized $\mathbf{LVP}@\mathbf{M}-101$ nanocomposites as cathode materials in LIBs was investigated. Even after 1000 cycles, a high reversible capacity of 113.1 and $105.8\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ at rates of $0.5\mathrm{C}$ and 1 C, respectively, was displayed in addition to its superior rate performance at various current densities from $0.1\mathrm{C}$ to $10\mathrm{C}$ . However, the authors stated that the relatively low initial CE $(87\%)$ of this material was due to electrolyte decomposition when forming the solid electrolyte interphase (SEI) layers and interfacial lithium storage.
Similarly, a one-pot in situ synthesis method was reported using GFDW (glass fiber drawing wastewater) that contained several organic linkers to initially fabricate vanadium-based metal–organic frameworks (V-MOFs), which were later applied as precursors to synthesize $\mathrm{Li}_{3}\mathrm{V}_{2}(\mathrm{PO}_{4})_{3}/$ phosphorus-doped carbon nanocomposites (simplified as LVP/P–C) with multilevel structures viz. spheroidal, foam, prism and flower-like structures [39]. GFDW was used as the source of organic ligands and carbon to obtain hybrid V-MOFs, which were annealed in situ to synthesize the various morphologies of phosphorus-doped carbon nanocomposite coatings on the surface of LVP particles. Wastewater generated in the glass fiber spinning process is called GFDW, and its treatment is a major challenge that limits the sustainable development of the glass fiber industry. It contains impregnating compounds, lipids, emulsifiers and dissolved organic matter, which are difficult and expensive to recover or treat. Furthermore, GFDW is directly discharged into the environment and has a high chemical oxygen demand (COD) and biological oxygen demand (BOD); thus, GFDW not only pollutes the environment but also threatens human health. Keeping this in mind, the authors used GFDW as a source for organic ligands and carbon to tackle this environmental issue and synthesize V-MOFs through a hydrothermal-assisted sol–gel method with a subsequent thermal treatment at $750^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in a nitrogen atmosphere to obtain multilevel-structured LVP/P–C nanocomposites. When used as LIB cathodes, the nanocomposites exhibited a long service life and a discharge capacity of $65\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $10\mathrm{C}$ with $90\%$ capacity retention even after 1100 cycles. The synthesis strategy presented in this study was not only easy, tunable and cost-effective but also contributed greatly to reducing the GFDW load on our environment. Chen’s group reported a facile, low-cost and scalable route for the synthesis of bimetallic PTA-based MOFs (Ni–MnPTA) in aqueous solution at room temperature. Subsequently, the bimetallic MOF was subjected to a two-step thermal treatment where primary Ni–Mn–O nanoparticles with sizes of $20{-}30~\mathrm{nm}$ were obtained during the first heat treatment (Fig. 13b) before being further subjected to high-temperature calcination in the presence of lithium salts to finally form a homogenous $\mathrm{LiNi_{0.5}M n_{1.5}O_{4}}$ material with high crystallinity [104]. As a cathode material for lithium-ion batteries, the as-synthesized ternary metal oxide $\mathrm{LiNi_{0.5}M n_{1.5}O_{4}}$ delivered a specific capacity of $115.9\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ at $20\mathrm{C}$ and retained $83.8\%$ of its capacity after 500 cycles. Similarly, Yin et al. reported the synthesis of a series of $\mathrm{LiNi_{0.5}M n_{1.5}O_{4}}$ (LNMO) materials. Because of their inferior ionic/electrical conductivity and high concentration of $\mathrm{Mn}^{3+}$ on the surface, a low specific capacitance and poor rate/cycling performance were achieved, impeding the use of these materials as cathodes in lithium-ion batteries [58]. However, spinel $\mathrm{LiNi_{0.5}M n_{1.5}O_{4}}$ has been increasingly studied due to its wide working voltage, high energy density, low cost, natural abundance and environmental affordability. The authors demonstrated the synthesis of a series of LNMO with a controlled $\mathrm{Mn}^{3+}$ concentration. This synthesis involved the hydrothermal method coupled with the high-temperature calcination of six Ni–MnMOFs, where the metal salts were common and the organic ligands were different, viz. P-phthalic acid (PTA), trimeric acid (TCA), 2,5-dihydroxyterephthalic acid (DTA), $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -oxybisbenzoic acid (OBA), $^{3,3^{\prime},4,4^{\prime}}$ -biphenyltetracarboxylic acid (BCA) and 3,4,9,10-perylenetetracarboxylic acid (PTCDA). After a thorough investigation, the experimental results revealed that the electrochemical properties were influenced by the morphology, sizes and composition of the MOFs. The authors also stated three important factors, namely, (1) the $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{n}}^{3+}$ concentration on the material surface, (2) the presence of impurity phases and (3) the crystal orientation that governed the overall electrochemical properties of spinel LNMO. The lowest impurity contents were observed for the PTCDALNMO and PTA-LNMO samples, which benefited their electrochemical properties over other candidates. Among all cathodes, PTCDA–Ni–Mn-MOF-derived spinel LNMO exhibited the highest initial discharge capacity of $145.6\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ at a 1 C rate with Coulombic efficiency of $89.6\%$ . This promising cathode material also exhibited good elevated temperature $(40^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ performance with a capacity retention of $94.4\%$ after 500 cycles at a $1\mathrm{C}$ rate. Furthermore, Wei et al. reported the MOF-assisted solvothermal method to deposit a suitable amount of zinc into layered Li-rich $\mathrm{Li_{1.2}M n_{0.54}N i_{0.13}C o_{0.13}O_{2}}$ to finally form mesoporous nano-microparticles of lithiumrich $Z\mathrm{n}$ -doped $\mathrm{Li}_{1.2}\mathrm{Mn}_{0.54}\mathrm{Co}_{0.13}\mathrm{Ni}_{0.13}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ [57]. According to $\mathrm{Li_{1.2}M n_{0.54}C o_{0.13}N i_{0.13}Z n_{\it x}O_{2}}$ $\scriptstyle\left(x=0\right.$ , 0.02, 0.04), the LIB cathode material containing a stoichiometric ratio of $0.02\ {\mathrm{Zn}}$ delivered an initial discharge capacity of $204.6\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ at $1\mathrm{C}$ and maintained a capacity retention of $82\%$ $(167.8~\mathrm{mAh~g^{-1}},$ at the 100th cycle. Stoichiometric ratios of 0 and $0.04\ Z\mathrm{n}$ delivered initial discharge capacities of $198.4\:\mathrm{mAh\:g^{-1}}$ and $196.2\mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively, and maintained capacity retention of $76\%$ ( $\mathrm{150.8\mAh\g^{-1}},$ ) and $78\%$ ( $153.0\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}},$ ), respectively, at the 100th cycle. A suitable amount of zinc substituted into the layered Li-rich $\mathrm{Li_{1.2}M n_{0.54}N i_{0.13}C o_{0.13}O_{2}}$ significantly improved the electrochemical performance of the material; in addition, different degrees of the homogeneous structural integration of the layered Li-rich character of $\mathrm{Li_{1.2}M n_{0.54}N i_{0.13}C o_{0.13}O_{2}}$ would be achieved by doping with different amounts of zinc. The wellcrystallized nano-microstructures were beneficial for reducing the electron and/or lithium diffusion lengths, facilitating a suitable contact area for connecting the active materials with the electrolyte, providing efficient diffusion into the inner regions of the electrode, and tolerating the volume fluctuations accompanied by repeated ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ insertion and extraction.
Zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs) are another type of attractive precursor for the derivation of porous carbon and/or nitrogen-doped porous carbon materials (NPCs) and several metallic compounds (MCs). The research groups of Cheng et al. and Zhao et al. successfully reported ZIF8-derived N-doped carbon and 33D microporous carbon (3D-C), which served as cathodes for lithium-ion capacitors (LICs) and lithium-ion batteries, respectively [105, 106]. The results discussed here showed the diversity of MOF-mediated in situ wrapping reactions and the construction of microporous structure-confined nanomaterials for high-energy electrochemical applications. Furthermore, to advance the preexisting methodologies and to stop capacity fading while cycling, Zhao et al. reported a facile preparation process for anthraquinone (AQ)-based LIB cathode materials [40]. However, anthraquinone (AQ) has also been tested as an organic cathode material for lithium-ion batteries because of its two-electron reduction property, high theoretical capacity and good reversible electrochemical behavior. This material has excellent potential to update preexisting energy storage technologies owing to its facile and desirable preparation at the molecular level, high theoretical gravimetric capacities and incredible sustainability. The detailed synthesis methodology involved the initial synthesis of ZIF-8 and the subsequent annealing at $1000^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $180~\mathrm{{min}}$ in an Ar atmosphere to finally yield an N-ZIF-8 MOF. Later, by using the melt-vaporization method, N-ZIF-8 and AQ were uniformly mixed with the help of a planetary ball mill followed by annealing at $300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to obtain AQ@N-ZIF-8 (Fig. 14). The as-yielded LIB cathode material delivered an initial discharge capacity of $240\mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ at a $0.1\mathrm{C}$ rate, and had capacity retention of up to $216\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ after 300 cycles with very low capacity fading of approximately $0.08\%$ per cycle. The high lithium-ion storage capacity of the prepared cathode was mainly attributed to the (1) microporous structure with well-distributed porosity, (2) synergistic effects of rational design, (3) high loading capacity of AQ encapsulation, (4) enhanced conductivity of the encapsulated AQ due to high contents of the nitrogen dopant and (5) nanopore confinement of AQ dissolution in carbon scaffolds.
Lin’s research group has made considerable efforts to develop ZIF-derived cathode materials for lithium-ion battery applications. Initially, the group reported lithium cobaltate nanoparticles derived from annealing the ZIF-67 precursor and $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{CO}_{3}$ in air, together with a homogeneous ${\mathrm{AlF}}_{3}$ coating and carbon nanotube (CNT) wrapping [107]. The as-synthesized $\mathrm{LiCoO_{2}/C N T s}@\mathrm{AlF_{3}}$ acted as a promising cathode at both room temperature and an elevated temperature of $50^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for enhanced lithium storage. The cathode exhibited an initial discharge capacity of $134.9\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ at a $1\mathrm{C}$ rate, and capacity retention of up to $120\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ after 200 cycles. This outstanding performance was attributed to the (1) uniform elemental distribution, high crystallinity, and N doping of the developed cathode material, possibly due to the strong complexation between the metal ions and organic ligands in the ZIF-67 MOF precursor; (2) introduction of CNTs, employed as rapid electron channels for transportation; (3) surface modification of ${\mathrm{AlF}}_{3}$ , preventing Co ions from being dissolved in the electrolyte during cycling; and (4) surface processes that dominated capacitance contributions to realize fast reaction kinetics and enhanced cycling performance stability.
Furthermore, the in situ growth of hexagonal-layered $\mathbf{LiCoO}_{2}$ on a 3D Au-coated copper foam (ACF) substrate (simplified as LCO@ACF) was reported, where the synthesis strategy involved the microwave-assisted doping of lithium ions to ZIF-67 $\mathrm{(Li@ZIF-67)}$ followed by in situ growth on the ACF substrate to achieve the $\operatorname{Li@}Z\operatorname{IF-67}\textcircled{a}$ ACF precursor [108]. Finally, the as-prepared precursor acted as a self-sacrificing template and was subjected to annealing under optimal conditions to finally obtain selfstanding 3 $\operatorname{DLCO}@\mathcal{A}$ CF. This efficient 3D self-supported cathode was connected in an LCO $@$ ACF//graphite full cell and exhibited distinguished electrochemical performances even at $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . As an LIB half-cell at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the $\operatorname{LCO}@$ ACF electrode delivered notable cycling stability of $136.4\ \mathrm{mAh}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at a $2\mathrm{~C~}$ rate even after 600 cycles; furthermore, LCO $@$ ACF had an excellent rate capability of $111.7\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}$ at $15~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Fig. 15). Here, ACF served as (1) the backbone, (2) the  electron pathway for charge delivery and storage and (3) the active material support. As depicted in Fig. 15d, the lithiation/delithiation reaction mechanism was proposed for the practical LIB full-cell device constructed with commercial graphite as the anode and the as-synthesized $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{LCO}}@\mathrm{ACF}$ as the cathode. Li ions were extracted from the layered $\mathbf{LiCoO}_{2}$ and intercalated into graphite during the charge process, while they transferred back during the discharge process. The notable electrochemical performance of the self-standing 3D LCO $@$ ACF cathode in advanced LIBs might be attributed to the structural features of the MOF precursor and 3D substrate, larger porosity and improved electrochemical conductivity. In another study, the authors proposed a novel route for the synthesis of the in situ construction of a MOF-derived nitrogen-doped carbon-encapsulated $\mathbf{LiCoO}_{2}$ heterostructure (denoted as $\operatorname{LCO}@\operatorname{C})$ by elevated-temperature solid-state annealing [109]. The annealing of ZIF-67 was performed at three different temperatures, and the N-doped cathode derived at $700~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (denoted as $\scriptstyle\mathrm{LCO}@\mathbf{C}-700)$ ) delivered long-term cycling stability (a gravimetric/areal capacity of $171.1\mathrm{\mAh}$ $\mathrm{g}^{-1}/4.2\ \mathrm{mAh\cm}^{-2}$ at $\mathrm{~1~C~}$ after 200 cycles) and superior rate capability (a high capacity of $150.3\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ even at a harsh current density of $10\mathrm{C})$ in the voltage range of $3.0{-}4.5\mathrm{V}.$ The long-term cycling stability and superior rate capability were mainly attributed to the carbon coating, which reduced the contact between $\mathbf{LiCoO}_{2}$ and the electrolyte and provided efficient diffusion paths; additionally, the strong interaction between the carbon and LCO bulk improved the electrochemical conductivity and structural strain distortion during the cycling process. A bimetallic ZIF-derived $\mathrm{CoF}_{2}/\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ hybrid structure was also reported by the group via simple copyrolysis with $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ [52]. First, the Fe–Co-ZIF precursor was synthesized by a time-saving and facile stirring method and later subjected to a twostep thermal decomposition. During the first step of thermal treatment, the organic ligand was converted into a conductive 3D porous graphitized framework $\scriptstyle(\mathrm{Fe}/\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{C})$ , and in the second step, copyrolysis with $\mathrm{NH}_{4}\mathrm{F}$ generated the hybrid $\mathrm{CoF}_{2}/\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ material. The in situ generated C/N-doped $\mathrm{CoF}_{2}/\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ framework showed an effectively increased conductivity and provided more ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ -active insertion sites, consequently demonstrating an improved electrochemical performance of $130.4\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ after 400 cycles at a current density of $100\ \mathrm{mA\g^{-1}}$ .
## 5  Conclusions and Perspectives
Lithium-ion battery cathode materials, as a new type of environmentally friendly energy storage material, are urgently needed in the rapidly expanding market due to growing energy demands. Many more research efforts are required to design and explore new cathode materials for lithium-ion batteries with better microstructures and macroscopic properties. Looking at the development trend of current research, we believe that MOF materials have great application prospects in the fields of energy storage and conversion.
First, the main considerations of the direct utilization of MOFs to function as cathode materials are as follows: (1) to provide more lithium storage sites by using metal clusters or organic groups with redox activity; (2) to improve the electrochemical reaction activity and prevent structural degradation by adapting conductive and rigid framework structures; and (3) to use robust and porous MOFs to provide a better method for lithium-ion intercalation or deintercalation, which circumvents unwanted side reactions. In addition to the direct application of MOFs as LIB cathodes, an important research direction is the use of MOFs as precursors or modifiers during the synthesis of composite cathode materials to provide superior electrochemical performance and better applicability than the parent MOFs. The following solutions may be considered when developing MOF-derived materials as cathodes. (1) To improve the electrical conductivity of the material, conductive materials, such as graphene, carbon fiber, carbon nanotubes, copper foam and nickel foam, can be introduced. (2) To provide a protective layer for the active electrode material via surface modification/coating is a worthwhile approach to reduce the electrolytic dissolution of active electrode material and enhance the cycling stability. (3) To improve the low initial Coulombic efficiency of MOF derivatives because of side reactions originating from the decomposition of electrolyte, effective strategies, such as the prelithiation techniques in surface chemistry, can be practically applied. (4) Make full use of the porosity and high specific surface area of active materials to increase the contact area between electrolytes and electrode materials, which favors Li-ion intercalation/deintercalation. Fortunately, MOF template-mediated synthesis routes offer excellent morphological features, such as a sturdy framework structure to enhance the electrochemical reaction and to withstand structural degradation during the reaction. In addition, MOFs are excellent precursors as carbon sources, where they retain the pristine MOF morphology and porous structure even after harsh thermal treatment. Another prospect is the choice of ligand to generate heteroatom-doped porous carbon, in which 2-methylimidazole is a better organic ligand than other reported organic ligands to synthesize ZIFs and subsequently to generate heteroatom-doped porous carbonaceous materials and other metal components. Furthermore, we envisage that zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs) are promising candidates to better serve as precursors to synthesize highefficiency cathode materials for next-generation LIBs due to their controllable morphology, simple preparation and large-scale yield. However, the optimization of synthesis conditions is highly desirable for the large-scale synthesis of MOF-derived metal components. (5) Construct a hollow internal and/or hierarchical structure to provide abundant space, which can effectively buffer the volume expansion caused during the cycling process to ensure the integrity and stability of connections. (6) Produce a nanostructure with uniform/homogeneous porosity to provide continuous electron channels and greatly shorten the charge diffusion distance, which is the key factor affecting electrochemical performance.
At present, research on the application of MOFs and MOF-derived metal components in LIB cathode materials has made limited progress. To increase the synthesis yield, realize large-scale production and promote industrialization, a reduction in the preparation cost of cathode materials derived from MOFs is required. Active research on interfacial engineering enables new ideas for protecting active electrode materials from breakdown during battery cycling. Approaches that use protective coatings have already been successfully implemented to reinforce the SEI layer. Furthermore, the utilization of advanced testing tools such as X-ray diffraction, X-ray diffraction microtomography (XRD-CT), neutron powder diffraction (NPD), scanning electron microscopy (SEM), tunneling electron microscopy (TEM), X-ray microscopy, transmission X-ray microscopy (TXM) and electrochemical strain microscopy (ESM) may provide more insight into understanding and/or assessing electrode material degradation during cell operation. The implementation of these advanced characterization techniques will greatly benefit a plethora of future innovative strategies to enhance the cycling life and safety of Li-ion batteries [110].
